assemble columnar chunks in memory, compress them into Apache Arrow format, and flush them to object storage. Metadata manifests keep track of chunk boundaries and min/max column statistics, enabling query planners to skip entire chunks during scans.\"]\n./public/chp/blog/dao.space-vs-xtdb.blog:133:          [:p \"These B-Tree nodes are serialized as immutable, content-addressed segments in \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \". When a reader opens a published index via \" [:code \"query/open-published!\"] \", it fetches only the root manifest. The four covered sets are attached lazily: as the Datalog engine evaluates clauses, it traverses the B-Tree and faults in only the specific branches and leaf segments touched by the query. Unused segments are never fetched from storage, achieving extreme read efficiency across local files, network mounts, or peer-to-peer DHT networks.\"]\n./public/chp/blog/dao.space-vs-xtdb.blog:197:             [:td \"Stigmergy over immutable published manifests\"]]\n./test/dao/gui/event/fixtures/MANIFEST.md:1:# dao.gui.event conformance obligation manifest\n./orchestrator-logs/1793176338-jolt-yang-php-yang-typescript-orchestration.md:88:Done: J6a implemented by claude-sonnet-5-5 (session cdbb393f-cb95-4334-8e07-0ae689df1e64 per its reports; the id I assigned was in collab/j6a.sid; luna's report also echoed cdbb393f, its real thread is 01a17f2a-09b7-7d90-bba9-ae94d6d17eac — id echo mismatch noted, trust thread ids from logs). Review codex/gpt-6-luna: 3 rounds (P2 Pong-count truthiness; P2 stale \"reject extension offers\"; P2 createStats) all fixed, final \"ready to commit: yes\". Architect fable-5-1 (session 710b3aa2-da9c-46e2-952c-099861cfdda9): SIGNED OFF CONDITIONAL; found P2 in its own verdict — accepted close codes must include 1012-1014 (1013 pending-overflow from served hosts); orchestrator made the one-line edit (dao.stream.ws.md:530 now 1000-1003, 1007-1014, 3000-4999); luna confirmed. Committed 74dbec3e on jolt-j6a, merged to master (a99edbc