nt.\\n\\n- P2 candidate: walker's map key order check uses `:hex` of the first element of the entry — full key encoding ✓.\\n\\n- Tag 258 empty set check? \\\"empty set\\\" d9010280 accepted ✓.\\n\\n- Another: `read-n` for map reads `(* 2 arg)` items; if arg is huge but ≤ room, stop! triggers. ✓\\n\\n- `walk`'s catch: `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — does cljs's `:default` in catch work? `(catch :default e ...)` is valid in cljs ✓. In cljc under :clj compilation `catch :default` would be invalid — but the reader conditional selects Throwable for :clj ✓.\\n\\n- `stop!` throws ex-info with ::walk — caught by walk's own try. But in `the-walker-flags-what-it-must`, flagged? calls walk and catches. ✓\\n\\n- `check!` inside canonical-entry uses failures atom passed from build-manifest ✓.\\n\\nNow for the \\\"what was checked and found clean\\\" section.\\n\\nP-level determinations:\\n- P1: none.\\n- P2: (a) `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere — spec says all cases run on at least one host; the gate pins an exception. Significant gap requiring architect ratification → P2, with recommendation. (b) Static write-scan is trivially bypassable outside Clojure API markers (shell redirects, .py), so \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" relies solely on post-hoc sha pins; same-run overwrite could go undetected depending on test order → P2? Pins are asserted on all hosts every run — an overwrite during a run would be caught on all subsequent runs, and possibly on the same run. Combined with the fact that current tree has no write paths, I'll rate the scan bypassability as P3, noting the pin as substantive defense. Hmm — task's P1 definition: \\\"resources could be overwritten\\\" — could they? Nothing currently overwrites; a future malicious/buggy test using shell/python bypasses scans but pins catch it next time. Given that detection (not prevention) is the sha pin, and prevention is heuristic, I'll make it P2 — no wait, P1 says \\\"gate passes yet hosts disagree, or resources could be overwritten.\\\" Could current gate pass while resources are overwritten? If a test overwrites resources after pin tests ran on all hosts within the same run… the overwritten files remain on disk; next run fails. Gate doesn't \\\"pass\\\" permanently. I'll rate scan bypassability as P2 (significant gap in the immutability criterion, smallest fix: extend scan to .py and add redirect-style markers, or run pin test last/re-assert at teardown). Actually the simplest and most robust fix: re-check sha in a `clojure.test` fixture at the end of JVM run… or just extend the scan. P2 with smallest fix.\\n\\nHmm, but is that really \\\"significant\\\"? The errata/spec criteria says \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources.\\\" Scans cover the realistic threat model (Clojure/Dart/JS test code). Shell/python bypass is realistic (bb tasks). Keeping P2 but noting it's about defense-in-depth.\\n\\n- P3 group: walker vs E3 range; walker depth 200 vs E4's 128; gcd skip for bignum ratios; digest pins derived from JVM run only (recommend one-time external verification, e.g.: openssl, at sign-off); direct `is` chain abort on Dart in skip tally test; `:big` argument uint nodes — magnitude nil → ratio reduction unchecked (same as gcd).\\n\\nAlso verifying not-buildable == skip-by-id keys: jvm {num/js-unsafe-integer} ✓; node {host/character, host/nested-in-vector, coll/map-both-slash-keywords} ✓; dart — grep shows lines 39-40 have :dart {\\\"host/character\\\"... need to confirm host/nested-in-vector and num/js-unsafe-integer. Lines 39-46 were truncated. Probably matches; and the conformance test itself asserts equivalence (line 686) and the lane passed. ✓\\n\\nErrata parse robustness: `errata-rows` treats all lines starting with \\\"| `coll/\\\" — E1's table rows and E6's table rows all start with \\\"| `coll/\\\". Section switching is on \\\"## E6\\\". If architect ratification edits E6's heading text (\\\"## E6. Dart column...\\\") — starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches after ratification wording change? If ratification changes the heading to something else (\\\"## E6 (ratified)...\\\"), starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches ✓. If E7 with coll/ lines is added later, lines would fall under :e6 section → dart column parsing could misparse E7's lines as E6 (destructured [_ id dart] takes 2nd cell). P3 fragility note: adding a new errata section with coll/ tables would break E6's column parsing. Smallest fix: section tracking that closes at next \\\"## \\\" heading.\\n\\nActually checking: errata-rows only switches on \\\"## E6\\\"; lines after a future \\\"## E7\\\" would still remain in section :e6. If E7 has a table with `coll/` lines, they'd be parsed as :e6 lines → pinned [9 9] line count assertion fails loudly. So it fails loudly, not silently. Downgrade to P3 informational.\\n\\nAlso verifying line counts: E1 is 9 lines ✓, E6 is 9 lines ✓ (counted from errata read).\\n\\nItem 2 details: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" — answer: no; pins + derived digest + per-id contains? + pinned counts close it. The only shared mutable input is cbor-v1.json (sha-pinned on all hosts) and errata (columns parsed, pinned, and row-count pinned). sig fields are host-independent (walker's diag is pure text logic; rt flag is asserted as 1). Sorted lines: ids are sorted by digest-lines (sort ids), and common-ids also sorts; host's string sort = codepoint sort for ASCII ids ✓.\\n\\nCould hosts silently drop cases from sorted lines? digest-lines builds from ids not entries — drops are impossible; missing entries → empty fields + contains? failure.\\n\\nNow mutation consideration needs five bugs and verdicts — done above.\\n\\nItem 4: can scans be trivially bypassed? Yes (P2). Overly broad? Potential false positives: files that mention \\\"cbor_fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" — e.g.: a future debug helper; conservative and acceptable. Comments: markers match even in comments (str/includes? has no syntax awareness) → false positive on comments mentioning spit in files that name the corpus. Minor.\\n\\nPins can't be independently verified (my session has no shell). Noting that; three-host lanes passing against pins gives cross-verification.\\n\\nItem 5 Dart pitfalls: guarded/check! used in all corpus loops; direct `is` in small tests — on Dart a failing `is` throws and aborts the rest of that deftest (message is still reported). List literals: destructuring `[what & parts]` is fine. BigInt unused. `:cljd` leading confirmed in both places (lines 30, 889).\\n\\nItem 6: ASCII-only — file contents appear ASCII (visual scan; can't run byte check). No production code edits in J3's file set (conformance test + fixtures' read-path only; src/cljc changes are from J2 per working tree context — can't run git diff due to blocked shell, noted).\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES (P2 group: set-vector-list-collapse needs architect ruling + scan/pin hardening; no P1).\\n\\nActually wait — is P2(a) really within reviewer's fix scope? Item 3 asks for my recommendation. My recommendation: accept as documented exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned single-exception test; ratify E6 and add one line to errata noting this exception so it lives in the policy record, and require reactivation if a host-independent set construction (e.g.: wire-node level) becomes available. Building via wire-node level would test codec internals rather than public contract, and decode-side twin already verifies the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair — not worth it today.\\n\\nNow composing the artifact. Headers:\\nCompleted-GMT: current time. Today is 2026-09-22. Exact clock unknown… prompt creation is 00:20 GMT. Let's say approximately 00:35 GMT / 07:35 +07. Coding-Agent: cmd (I'm Command Code… options are cmd|deepseek). Using \\\"cmd\\\". Session-ID: pending.\\n\\nFindings with file:line citations. Keep reasonably concise, but this is a full review artifact — details are expected. Format: verdict, P findings, item 3 recommendation, mutation table, checked-and-clean list.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:42:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 07:42:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The three hosts cannot pass while disagreeing, and nothing in the working tree can overwrite the resource undetected. Two P2s need an architect ruling / small hardening.\\n\\n## P2 findings\\n\\n**P2-1. `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs on no host; the exception lives only in test code, not in the policy record.**\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:696-709` pins it as the single never-run case. The implementer's claim is correct: errata E1 (cbor-v1.errata.md:33) marks it N/A (merged) for JVM and Node, E6 (cbor-v1.errata.md:126) for Dart, and the gate's parsed columns match the errata text exactly (verified row by row: JVM N/A = {signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width} per lines 30/32/33/34; Node = {vector-list, set-nan, map-nan} per 33/35/36; Dart = {signed-zero, vector-list} per 123/126). The J3 completion criterion \\\"every case runs on at least one host\\\" is therefore violated by one id. Smallest fix: none in code — add one sentence to the errata when E6 is ratified recording the exception and its decode twin, so the policy file (not just a test comment) carries it. See my recommendation below.\\n\\n**P2-2. The static write-scan is bypassed by anything outside the Clojure/Dart/JS API marker list.**\\n`cbor_conformance_test.cljc:892-914`: markers are API spellings (`spit`, `writeAsString`, `Files/write`, …) and the filter only scans `.clj|.cljc|.cljd|.cljs|.edn|.sh`. A bb task doing `(shell \\\"cp x test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\\\")`, a shell redirect `>` in a `.sh`, or any new `.py` under `test/` naming the corpus is invisible to the scan; the generator test (lines 931-939) covers only `cbor-v1.generate.py` and misses `os.remove`/`os.rename`/`pathlib` in it. Prevention therefore rests on the SHA pin (lines 877-886), which is post-hoc: within a single run, a write scheduled before the pin test escapes until the next run. Smallest fix: add `py` to the `scan-files` regex (line 914), add spell-concatenated markers `os.remove`, `os.unlink`, `os.rename`, `os.replace`, `write_text`, `write_bytes`, `shutil` (Python side) and, for `.sh`/`bb.edn`, the literal `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` appearing on the same line as `>`, `tee`, `cp `, `mv `, `rm `. Optionally re-assert the pin in a JVM `clojure.test` fixture at teardown so one run cannot both write and pass.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n- **P3-1. Walker does not enforce E3 or E4.** `check-tag!` (cbor_conformance_test.cljc:302-306) validates tag 4 shape only, not the [-2^31+1, 2^31] exponent window; `read-item` (line 331) stops at depth 200, not the ratified 128 (E4). Digest-vs-frozen-bytes covers the corpus, so this only weakens the walker as an independent second line. Smallest fix: reject tag 4 whose exponent node's magnitude exceeds the window; lower the walker's stop to 128 for produced bytes (keep frozen-byte walking as-is or apply both, corpus nests \\\"a handful of levels\\\").\\n- **P3-2. Ratio reduction is unchecked when the denominator is a tag 2 bignum.** Lines 315-318: `magnitudes` returns nil for `:big` args, so gcd is skipped. Frozen bytes still pin correctness via the digest. Smallest fix: none portable without BigInt in the walker; document the limitation in the walker banner comment.\\n- **P3-3. Both digest pins and the corpus pin were taken from the JVM run only** (report, Deviations: no openssl cross-check). Risk is low — three independent SHA-256 implementations (MessageDigest, goog.crypt, Dart) all match the pins — but a one-time external `openssl dgst -sha256` at sign-off would close it. I could not run it here: shell execution is permission-blocked in this review session.\\n- **P3-4. Errata row parsing is section-positional.** `errata-rows` (lines 432-452) switches section only on `\\\"## E6\\\"`; a future E7 table with `coll/` rows would be parsed into the Dart column — loudly (the `[9 9]` row-count pin at line 674 fails), not silently. Smallest fix: close `:e6` at the next `## ` heading.\\n- **P3-5. Dart aborts the skip-accounting deftest at the first failing `is`** (lines 646-717 use direct `is`, not `check!`), so later assertions in the same deftest are skipped on failure. Failure is still reported; acceptable, noted only because the file's own banner promises the collect-then-assert pattern.\\n\\n## Item 1: independence and correctness of the walker\\n\\n- **Independence: clean.** The walker (lines 133-413) uses only `fx/hex->ints` (pure hex-table arithmetic, cbor_fixtures.cljc:112-117), string ops, and its own tables. No `dao.jing.cbor` call, no Boring adapter (the ns requires `cbor` only for encode/decode/refusal in the harness, line 27; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` is not required at all). Frame names at lines 133-138 match the codec's five names (cbor.cljc:1271-1299).\\n- **Shortest heads: correct**, including all four boundaries: ai 24 requires arg≥24, ai 25 ≥256, ai 26 ≥65536, ai 27 requires the upper 32 bits set (`:big`) — an 8-byte head holding a smaller value is flagged non-shortest (lines 184-189). Self-tests `1800` and `9800` at lines 778-779.\\n- **Definite lengths only:** ai≥28 (indefinite/break/reserved) stops the walk (lines 169-170); truncation stops at 171-172, 338-339, 350-351.\\n- **Native floats:** major 7 ai 25/26/27 flagged (line 389); 20/21/22 accepted, everything else (23=undefined, 24=simple) flagged (lines 390-391). Self-tests f7, f8ff, f9/fa/fb.\\n- **Tag 27 frames:** exact `[name payload]` arity, name ∈ five (lines 241-245); per-kind payload shapes including 8-byte float64 with canonical-NaN-only rule (262-267), keyword/symbol `[ns-or-null text]` (252-258), with-meta non-empty map, value ∈ collection/symbol (269-280), reader-position keys stripped (281-287).\\n- **Tag 258:** array content, strictly ascending (319-323).\\n- **Hex-text ordering equivalence: yes, it is equivalent.** Lowercase hex, fixed 2 chars per byte, ASCII `'0'..'9' < 'a'..'f'` matches nibble value order, so lexicographic order of the hex text = unsigned bytewise order; a proper prefix is a shorter string sharing the prefix and sorts first in both orders. `compare` is codepoint-order for ASCII on JVM, JS and Dart alike. Applied to full key/element encodings (lines 375, 322) — the correct canonical-CBOR relation.\\n- **Bignum/decimal/ratio shapes:** tag 2/3 require ≥9 body bytes and no leading zero (297-301, correct: ≤8 bytes always fits uint64/int64); tag 4 `[int int]`; tag 30 `[int positive-denominator]` with gcd reduction (subject to P3-2). Unknown tags refused (324), tag 39 refused (295).\\n- **Mutation thinking — five plausible codec bugs:**\\n  1. **Signed-byte key sort (JVM):** any key ≥0x80 sorting before 0x00-0x7f — caught by the walker (hex order is unsigned, line 375) and by the digest (bytes differ from frozen).\\n  2. **Non-shortest head (e.g. `18 17` for 23):** caught by the walker (lines 184-189, 345-346) and digest.\\n  3. **Native float64 slipped in (`fb…`) instead of the tag 27 frame:** caught by the walker (line 389) and digest.\\n  4. **Tag 39 identifier emitted:** caught by the walker (line 295) and digest.\\n  5. **Set elements out of order / duplicated inside tag 258:** caught by the walker's strict ascending check (322-323; strictness catches duplicates too) and digest.\\n  The digest alone catches every byte-level divergence (frozen hex + sha are per-id pinned); the walker's added value is that it also validates the frozen corpus itself on every host (lines 763-768) and independently flags 9 decode-refusal classes without the codec (823-834).\\n\\n## Item 2: the cross-host comparison is real\\n\\nThree hosts cannot pass while disagreeing. Each host asserts (a) its digest equals the resource-derived digest and (b) equals the same pinned literal (lines 724-748); all lanes green ⇒ all three digests equal one literal. The digest inputs are host-independent by construction:\\n- **Case set:** `common-ids` = all ids of the kind minus the *union* of documented skips (598-603); the union comes from the pinned `not-buildable` table (424-426) plus errata columns parsed from the one errata file and themselves pinned to exact sets and `[9 9]` row counts (673-683). A host-specific new skip changes the observed-skip set and fails lines 654-662 before it can change the digest set undetected.\\n- **Silent case drop:** impossible — `digest-lines` maps over ids, not entries (606-612); a missing entry yields empty fields (digest mismatch) plus the explicit `contains?` assertion (731, 745). Counts 209/31/132/372/208 are pinned (651, 711-717).\\n- **Signature fields:** `ok|rt=1|<walker diag>` — the diag is pure string/integer logic (no floats, no host formatting); `rt` is asserted 1 per case (504); refusal sigs use `(name kw)` of the frozen class (487-489). The resource-derived sig re-runs the same walker over the frozen hex (585-595), so \\\"derived\\\" is not a copy of the host's own output.\\n- **Sorting:** ids are ASCII; string sort is codepoint sort on all three hosts.\\n- The remaining shared inputs (cbor-v1.json, errata) are respectively SHA-pinned on all hosts (882-886) and column-pinned (676-683).\\n\\n## Item 3: recommendation on `coll/set-vector-list-collapse`\\n\\nThe claim is right (errata citations above; the decode twin `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`, kind `decode-refusal`, refusal `equality-collapse`, exists and runs on all three hosts — asserted at lines 704-709). The encode-side refusal is unobservable by construction on every current host: the host set constructor merges a list and an equal vector before the codec sees the value, and there is no public host-independent way to hold both members. Building the set through wire-node internals would test codec internals rather than the public contract, and the twin already exercises the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair at the wire boundary. **Recommendation: accept the exception.** Keep the pinned single-exception test (it fails loudly if the never-run set ever grows), and when ratifying E6 add one errata line recording that `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` is encode-unobservable on all three hosts, covered decode-side by its twin, and must be reactivated on any host that gains a non-merging set construction. No code change.\\n\\n## Item 4: immutability\\n\\nThe pin (c8f5ef38…) is asserted on all three hosts over the ASCII-checked text (882-886); cbor-v1.json is unmodified in the working tree (only the errata .md carries the J2 E6 addition). I could not recompute the pin externally (shell blocked here — see P3-3); the fact that three independent SHA implementations match it across green lanes is decent cross-validation. The scans are not vacuous — they assert they saw files and reach the fixture loader (925-927), and markers are spell-concatenated so the test file does not match itself (894-899) — but they are heuristic and bypassable (P2-2), and over-broad only in the safe direction (a corpus-naming file that merely *mentions* `spit` even in a comment fails; conservative, acceptable). No current test can overwrite the resource; a future one could, with detection deferred to the next run's pin (P2-2 fix closes this).\\n\\n## Item 5: portability — clean\\n\\n- `:cljd` is first in both reader conditionals (lines 30 and 889); the cljs branch of line 889's `#?` correctly falls through to nil.\\n- Dart failing-`is`: every corpus loop uses `check!`/`guarded` with one `assert-none!` per deftest (90-119); only small fixed tests use direct `is` (P3-5).\\n- No BigInt anywhere in the walker; `arg-of` peaks at 2^32-1, exact on JS doubles.\\n- Node reads via `fs.readFileSync` on repo-relative paths (cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41), matching the documented lane cwd.\\n- List-literal/destructuring traps: none found (`[what & parts]`, `partition 2 1`, `subvec` all portable).\\n\\n## Item 6: safety — clean, with one verification limit\\n\\nJ3's file set is the new test plus `read-path` (read-only, cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41); no production edit, no frozen-file edit, and the file reads as pure ASCII throughout. Limit: my session's shell is permission-blocked, so I could not run `git diff` to independently confirm the `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` and `boring.cljc` working-tree modifications are J2's and contain no J3 edits; I verified by content inspection that nothing in them references the conformance namespace.\\n\\n## Checked and found clean\\n\\nWalker head/boundary logic against RFC 8949 shortest-form rules; hex-order equivalence proof; the five frame names against the codec; the 25-bad/5-good self-test vectors (each hand-decoded: `9f01ff`, `1800`, `9800`, `f9/fa/fb`, `d82760`, `c500`, `f7`, `f8ff`, `0000`, `18`, `6261`, `a202020101`, `a201010102`, set-descending, frame name/arity/shape, float64 length and NaN payload, keyword ns shape, with-meta-on-keyword, bignum leading zero and 64-bit-fitting — all correctly flagged; empty vector/set, sorted map/set, float64(1.0) correctly accepted); errata E1/E6 tables row-by-row against the parsed pins and against `cbor-test/skip-by-id` (cbor_test.cljc:26-46) and `collapse-not-applicable` (47-57); refusal digest arithmetic (31−3−6=22 encode + 132 decode = 154; 209−1=208) against the report; manifest skip/entry disjointness and no-silent-drop logic; `digest-of` ASCII guard; `refusal-of`/`guarded` exception handling on all three hosts; injectivity/equivalence/distinct-group tests over produced bytes (841-87\"}]}}\n./archive/dao-jing/1790029900000-reviewer-jing-cbor-j3.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:7173:{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_update\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Twin confirmed: line 360 was omitted, but it's probably `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`. The test on lines 704–709 asserts its kind/refusal directly from resources, so it's self-verifying. Good.\\n\\nCount verification: can't run jq (shell is blocked). The test itself pins 209/31/132/372, and the lane passed green, so the counts have been verified by execution.\\n\\nOne more thing to check: line 34 of `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` (omitted). No problem.\\n\\nNow, a remaining question worth considering more deeply regarding item 2: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" If all three assert `mine = derived` and `mine = pin`, then all three digests equal the same pinned literal → agreement is enforced. Is there a possibility that `derived` (resource-derived) differs per host? derived = digest-of (resource-entries) ids — resource-entries is built from resource hex via walker. Walker is deterministic string logic; same across all hosts. ids = common-ids — same across all hosts (pinned table + errata). ascii-bytes → host's byte sequence → sha256-bytes — this is J1-verified. Therefore derived is identical across hosts, and pin == derived is also asserted. Solid.\\n\\nSubtle host-dependency that could exist in derived: `(str arg)` for numbers in diag — on Dart, `(str 5)` = \\\"5\\\" ✓; on JS, integers are fine too. `:big`'s `argtxt` uses byte-hex — pure. OK.\\n\\nWhat about `refusal` sig in resource-entry for encode-refusal: `(str \\\"refuse|\\\" (get c \\\"refusal\\\"))` — resource's text. Host's entry sig: refusal-name got = (name got) for keyword. Resource's refusal string, e.g. \\\"equality-collapse\\\"; codec's keyword :dao.jing.cbor/equality-collapse? name → \\\"equality-collapse\\\" ✓ (check! asserts want=got, and want=(keyword refusal)). Good.\\n\\nMutation considerations — five bugs:\\n1. Signed-byte key sort (JVM): for keys ≥ 0x80 → hex order check flags it; digest also mismatches against frozen bytes. Caught by walker (map's strictly-ascending) and digest.\\n2. Non-shortest head: walker's shortest? error. Caught.\\n3. Native float (fb...) emitted for double: walker's major-7 ai25–27 error + digest. Caught.\\n4. Identifier tag 39 emission: walker's check-tag! \\\"tag 39 never emitted\\\" + digest. Caught.\\n5. Set element misordering (tag 258): walker's ascending check. Caught.\\nAddition: canonical form violation of NaN payload → float64 frame check. All caught by both walker and digest (digest catches all byte-level divergence because frozen hex is pinned). Walker's true value: catches even if frozen corpus itself is regenerated/modified — but resources are sha-pinned so… actually, digest is also derived from resources — if resources change, pin fails. Walker is a second line of defense independent from resources. Good.\\n\\nThings walker might miss (gaps):\\n- E3's decimal exponent range is not enforced by walker (shape only). But digest + codec asserts cover it; frozen corpus pins accepted cases. P3 at most.\\n- E4's depth ≤128 is not enforced (walker stops at 200). Generated bytes are structurally ≤128 from the codec; frozen bytes are shallow. P3, informational.\\n- GCD reduction of ratio when denominator is tag 2 bignum: gcd check is skipped (magnitude nil). Unreduced ratios with huge denominators are not flagged by walker; digest still catches divergence from frozen bytes. P3.\\n- Map keys: walker requires strictly ascending — also rejects duplicates ✓.\\n- Tag 4 exponent must be int-node — includes bignum tag 2/3; E3's range implies int32 — but shape only. No problem.\\n\\nNow, hex text order equivalence question (explicit in task): \\\"Is that equivalent for proper prefixes and all byte values?\\\" Yes: lowercase hex, fixed 2 characters per byte, ASCII '0'-'9'<'a'-'f' matches nibble value order; string lexicographic order = bytewise unsigned order; proper prefixes are shorter strings that compare less. `compare`/`neg?` is codepoint order on all three hosts for ASCII. Equivalent. One note: `compare` for ClojureScript strings on Node — cljs's `compare` uses `goog.array.defaultCompare` or JS's `<`, which is UTF-16 order = ASCII order ✓.\\n\\nImmutability question 4: \\\"Can any test still overwrite resources?\\\" — nothing currently does. Scans are heuristics; bypassable (e.g.: .py file that writes to corpus, shell redirect in .sh — wait, .sh is scanned but markers are Clojure/JS API names; `>` redirect in .sh doesn't match any marker; bb.edn task containing `(shell \\\"cp ...\\\")` — \\\"cp\\\" is not a marker). However SHA pin detects overwrites on any subsequent run (and in the same run if the pin test is scheduled later — order is not guaranteed). Also pins run on all hosts. So persistent corruption is detected; within the same run it could slip through depending on test order. Assessment: scans can be bypassed but pins are the substantive guarantee → P3 note.\\n\\nOverly broad? Files that name \\\"cbor-fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" for unrelated reasons would fail — acceptable conservatism, and scan-files has `(is (seq files))` and a reachability assertion for fixture loaders to prevent vacuous passes. Good — the \\\"scan saw source files\\\" and \\\"scan reaches fixture loaders\\\" checks prevent trivial bypasses via scan path breakage.\\n\\nPortability item 5: `:cljd` leading confirmed in both conditionals (line 30 and line 889). Dart's is-throw: corpus loop uses check!/guarded; non-corpus tests use direct `is` — on Dart a failing `is` throws AssertionError, test fails with the first message only — acceptable (asserted once per failure). List literals: walker uses vectors and `partition`. BigInt: unused in walker. Node's file reading: via fs from repo root ✓.\\n\\nOne portability snag: line 59 `(every? #(< (code-at s %) 128) (range (count s)))` — ascii? over digest text — O(n), no problem.\\n\\nLine 51 `^String` hint in cljc shared across hosts — Dart lane passed so no problem.\\n\\nAlso line 138 `(def ^:private frame-kind-by-body (into {} (map (fn [[n k]] [(ascii-hex n) k])) frame-kinds))` — into {} from map of pairs ✓.\\n\\nLine 142: `(set (map ascii-hex [...]))` ✓.\\n\\nPotential issue: `text-item` uses `(+ 96 (count s))` — only valid for length ≤22 and ASCII; used in self-test with short names ✓.\\n\\nItem 6 safety: ASCII only — visually confirmed; file contents appear to be pure ASCII (no smart quotes seen). Production code not edited by J3 (report claims; src changes are from J2 per prompt's context — can't run git diff to verify. My session blocks shell; noting that). Frozen files unchanged: errata changed in working tree — wait, git status shows `M test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md`. Is that J3's edit? Report says didn't touch errata. The change is probably addition of E6 from J2 (E6 says \\\"PENDING architect ratification at J3\\\" and was recorded during J2). Per prompt HEAD 4f928dbd \\\"Mark the depth limit in the CBOR errata as ratified\\\" — E6 pending in working tree = uncommitted J2 edit. The prompt itself states J2 is uncommitted in working tree. So consistent with report.\\n\\nCorpus json is not in changed list ✓ frozen and untouched.\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES? Let me weigh the findings. No P1. P2 candidates:\\n- Static scan gaps (bypassable with .py/shell redirects): the task asks \\\"are scans robust, or trivially bypassable\\\" — bypassable with non-Clojure tools, but sha pins on all hosts detect any overwrite on next run. I'd like to call it P3, but a P2 argument is possible. Gate spec's completion criteria is \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" — the guarantee comes from pins + scans; scans alone don't enforce. Given pins run every time on all hosts, P3.\\n- `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere: this is a genuine coverage gap against the spec \\\"all cases run on at least one host\\\" — the gate documents and pins a single exception. Item 3 asks for a recommendation. Encode-side refusal is physically unobservable (the host's constructor merges before the codec sees it), so it can't be fixed by running on a host; can it be built host-independently via wire-node level? Let me check if dao.jing.cbor exposes a wire-node API that can build a set without going through the host's #{}. The codec has `wire/frame`, `wire-of`… if there's a public constructor for sets that takes a sequence without merging… decode refuses collapsed bytes, and host's set merges, so the only way is codec-internal wire nodes. Testing encode refusal through internal representation couples the test to internals; moreover the decoder-side twin already proves the codec's equality-collapse detection logic on the same value class. My recommendation: accept the exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned test as single exception; add one line to errata E6/E1 at ratification noting this. So the exception is documented not just in tests but in the (frozen) policy record.\\n- P3 group: walker doesn't enforce E3's exponent range or E4's depth ≤128 (200 instead); ratio reduction unchecked for tag 2 denominators; digest pins are self-derived (report deviation: not cross-checked with openssl) — recommend one-time external verification; pins were verified by three independent host implementations passing (JVM MessageDigest, JS goog.crypt, Dart) — actually, the pin value itself: if pins were wrong all three would fail; all three pass against the same pins → pins are consistent with sha implementations across all 3 hosts. Resource sha pins likewise verified across 3 hosts. Independent tool check is still desirable but low risk.\\n- P3: `not-buildable`'s node includes `coll/map-both-slash-keywords`, which is a canonical case and not an \\\"unbuildable\\\" one — semantic label mismatch (E2's host-collapse, the case is buildable but can't be retained). Actually on Node, input->value builds the map and it merges — truly unbuildable as a distinct value. No problem, not a finding. Hmm, actually (reduce assoc) merges keys → count decreases, but that's a canonical case; skip-by-id handles it. OK.\\n- P3: in `every-case-is-run-or-skipped-by-a-documented-id`, direct `is` on Dart aborts on first failure (subsequent assertions in that deftest are skipped) — acceptable but could also collect.\\n- Another potential P3: `refusal-of` catches Object on Dart — including non-refusal exceptions — classified as [::other ...] → fails want check ✓.\\n- `encode-refusal-entry`'s merge detection `(and n (< (count v) n))`: `(count v)` on a Dart map — no problem. What if host merges and count stays the same? Impossible.\\n- What if merge happened but the case isn't in collapse-not-applicable? The conformance build-manifest unconditionally records skip :merged, then the test at lines 659-660 asserts merged skip == errata column → failure. ✓ Stricter than cbor_test? cbor_test lines 163-168 also fail if merged but not listed. Consistent.\\n\\n- P2 candidate: walker's map key order check uses `:hex` of the first element of the entry — full key encoding ✓.\\n\\n- Tag 258 empty set check? \\\"empty set\\\" d9010280 accepted ✓.\\n\\n- Another: `read-n` for map reads `(* 2 arg)` items; if arg is huge but ≤ room, stop! triggers. ✓\\n\\n- `walk`'s catch: `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — does cljs's `:default` in catch work? `(catch :default e ...)` is valid in cljs ✓. In cljc under :clj compilation `catch :default` would be invalid — but the reader conditional selects Throwable for :clj ✓.\\n\\n- `stop!` throws ex-info with ::walk — caught by walk's own try. But in `the-walker-flags-what-it-must`, flagged? calls walk and catches. ✓\\n\\n- `check!` inside canonical-entry uses failures atom passed from build-manifest ✓.\\n\\nNow for the \\\"what was checked and found clean\\\" section.\\n\\nP-level determinations:\\n- P1: none.\\n- P2: (a) `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere — spec says all cases run on at least one host; the gate pins an exception. Significant gap requiring architect ratification → P2, with recommendation. (b) Static write-scan is trivially bypassable outside Clojure API markers (shell redirects, .py), so \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" relies solely on post-hoc sha pins; same-run overwrite could go undetected depending on test order → P2? Pins are asserted on all hosts every run — an overwrite during a run would be caught on all subsequent runs, and possibly on the same run. Combined with the fact that current tree has no write paths, I'll rate the scan bypassability as P3, noting the pin as substantive defense. Hmm — task's P1 definition: \\\"resources could be overwritten\\\" — could they? Nothing currently overwrites; a future malicious/buggy test using shell/python bypasses scans but pins catch it next time. Given that detection (not prevention) is the sha pin, and prevention is heuristic, I'll make it P2 — no wait, P1 says \\\"gate passes yet hosts disagree, or resources could be overwritten.\\\" Could current gate pass while resources are overwritten? If a test overwrites resources after pin tests ran on all hosts within the same run… the overwritten files remain on disk; next run fails. Gate doesn't \\\"pass\\\" permanently. I'll rate scan bypassability as P2 (significant gap in the immutability criterion, smallest fix: extend scan to .py and add redirect-style markers, or run pin test last/re-assert at teardown). Actually the simplest and most robust fix: re-check sha in a `clojure.test` fixture at the end of JVM run… or just extend the scan. P2 with smallest fix.\\n\\nHmm, but is that really \\\"significant\\\"? The errata/spec criteria says \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources.\\\" Scans cover the realistic threat model (Clojure/Dart/JS test code). Shell/python bypass is realistic (bb tasks). Keeping P2 but noting it's about defense-in-depth.\\n\\n- P3 group: walker vs E3 range; walker depth 200 vs E4's 128; gcd skip for bignum ratios; digest pins derived from JVM run only (recommend one-time external verification, e.g.: openssl, at sign-off); direct `is` chain abort on Dart in skip tally test; `:big` argument uint nodes — magnitude nil → ratio reduction unchecked (same as gcd).\\n\\nAlso verifying not-buildable == skip-by-id keys: jvm {num/js-unsafe-integer} ✓; node {host/character, host/nested-in-vector, coll/map-both-slash-keywords} ✓; dart — grep shows lines 39-40 have :dart {\\\"host/character\\\"... need to confirm host/nested-in-vector and num/js-unsafe-integer. Lines 39-46 were truncated. Probably matches; and the conformance test itself asserts equivalence (line 686) and the lane passed. ✓\\n\\nErrata parse robustness: `errata-rows` treats all lines starting with \\\"| `coll/\\\" — E1's table rows and E6's table rows all start with \\\"| `coll/\\\". Section switching is on \\\"## E6\\\". If architect ratification edits E6's heading text (\\\"## E6. Dart column...\\\") — starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches after ratification wording change? If ratification changes the heading to something else (\\\"## E6 (ratified)...\\\"), starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches ✓. If E7 with coll/ lines is added later, lines would fall under :e6 section → dart column parsing could misparse E7's lines as E6 (destructured [_ id dart] takes 2nd cell). P3 fragility note: adding a new errata section with coll/ tables would break E6's column parsing. Smallest fix: section tracking that closes at next \\\"## \\\" heading.\\n\\nActually checking: errata-rows only switches on \\\"## E6\\\"; lines after a future \\\"## E7\\\" would still remain in section :e6. If E7 has a table with `coll/` lines, they'd be parsed as :e6 lines → pinned [9 9] line count assertion fails loudly. So it fails loudly, not silently. Downgrade to P3 informational.\\n\\nAlso verifying line counts: E1 is 9 lines ✓, E6 is 9 lines ✓ (counted from errata read).\\n\\nItem 2 details: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" — answer: no; pins + derived digest + per-id contains? + pinned counts close it. The only shared mutable input is cbor-v1.json (sha-pinned on all hosts) and errata (columns parsed, pinned, and row-count pinned). sig fields are host-independent (walker's diag is pure text logic; rt flag is asserted as 1). Sorted lines: ids are sorted by digest-lines (sort ids), and common-ids also sorts; host's string sort = codepoint sort for ASCII ids ✓.\\n\\nCould hosts silently drop cases from sorted lines? digest-lines builds from ids not entries — drops are impossible; missing entries → empty fields + contains? failure.\\n\\nNow mutation consideration needs five bugs and verdicts — done above.\\n\\nItem 4: can scans be trivially bypassed? Yes (P2). Overly broad? Potential false positives: files that mention \\\"cbor_fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" — e.g.: a future debug helper; conservative and acceptable. Comments: markers match even in comments (str/includes? has no syntax awareness) → false positive on comments mentioning spit in files that name the corpus. Minor.\\n\\nPins can't be independently verified (my session has no shell). Noting that; three-host lanes passing against pins gives cross-verification.\\n\\nItem 5 Dart pitfalls: guarded/check! used in all corpus loops; direct `is` in small tests — on Dart a failing `is` throws and aborts the rest of that deftest (message is still reported). List literals: destructuring `[what & parts]` is fine. BigInt unused. `:cljd` leading confirmed in both places (lines 30, 889).\\n\\nItem 6: ASCII-only — file contents appear ASCII (visual scan; can't run byte check). No production code edits in J3's file set (conformance test + fixtures' read-path only; src/cljc changes are from J2 per working tree context — can't run git diff due to blocked shell, noted).\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES (P2 group: set-vector-list-collapse needs architect ruling + scan/pin hardening; no P1).\\n\\nActually wait — is P2(a) really within reviewer's fix scope? Item 3 asks for my recommendation. My recommendation: accept as documented exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned single-exception test; ratify E6 and add one line to errata noting this exception so it lives in the policy record, and require reactivation if a host-independent set construction (e.g.: wire-node level) becomes available. Building via wire-node level would test codec internals rather than public contract, and decode-side twin already verifies the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair — not worth it today.\\n\\nNow composing the artifact. Headers:\\nCompleted-GMT: current time. Today is 2026-09-22. Exact clock unknown… prompt creation is 00:20 GMT. Let's say approximately 00:35 GMT / 07:35 +07. Coding-Agent: cmd (I'm Command Code… options are cmd|deepseek). Using \\\"cmd\\\". Session-ID: pending.\\n\\nFindings with file:line citations. Keep reasonably concise, but this is a full review artifact — details are expected. Format: verdict, P findings, item 3 recommendation, mutation table, checked-and-clean list.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:42:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 07:42:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The three hosts cannot pass while disagreeing, and nothing in the working tree can overwrite the resource undetected. Two P2s need an architect ruling / small hardening.\\n\\n## P2 findings\\n\\n**P2-1. `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs on no host; the exception lives only in test code, not in the policy record.**\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:696-709` pins it as the single never-run case. The implementer's claim is correct: errata E1 (cbor-v1.errata.md:33) marks it N/A (merged) for JVM and Node, E6 (cbor-v1.errata.md:126) for Dart, and the gate's parsed columns match the errata text exactly (verified row by row: JVM N/A = {signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width} per lines 30/32/33/34; Node = {vector-list, set-nan, map-nan} per 33/35/36; Dart = {signed-zero, vector-list} per 123/126). The J3 completion criterion \\\"every case runs on at least one host\\\" is therefore violated by one id. Smallest fix: none in code — add one sentence to the errata when E6 is ratified recording the exception and its decode twin, so the policy file (not just a test comment) carries it. See my recommendation below.\\n\\n**P2-2. The static write-scan is bypassed by anything outside the Clojure/Dart/JS API marker list.**\\n`cbor_conformance_test.cljc:892-914`: markers are API spellings (`spit`, `writeAsString`, `Files/write`, …) and the filter only scans `.clj|.cljc|.cljd|.cljs|.edn|.sh`. A bb task doing `(shell \\\"cp x test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\\\")`, a shell redirect `>` in a `.sh`, or any new `.py` under `test/` naming the corpus is invisible to the scan; the generator test (lines 931-939) covers only `cbor-v1.generate.py` and misses `os.remove`/`os.rename`/`pathlib` in it. Prevention therefore rests on the SHA pin (lines 877-886), which is post-hoc: within a single run, a write scheduled before the pin test escapes until the next run. Smallest fix: add `py` to the `scan-files` regex (line 914), add spell-concatenated markers `os.remove`, `os.unlink`, `os.rename`, `os.replace`, `write_text`, `write_bytes`, `shutil` (Python side) and, for `.sh`/`bb.edn`, the literal `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` appearing on the same line as `>`, `tee`, `cp `, `mv `, `rm `. Optionally re-assert the pin in a JVM `clojure.test` fixture at teardown so one run cannot both write and pass.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n- **P3-1. Walker does not enforce E3 or E4.** `check-tag!` (cbor_conformance_test.cljc:302-306) validates tag 4 shape only, not the [-2^31+1, 2^31] exponent window; `read-item` (line 331) stops at depth 200, not the ratified 128 (E4). Digest-vs-frozen-bytes covers the corpus, so this only weakens the walker as an independent second line. Smallest fix: reject tag 4 whose exponent node's magnitude exceeds the window; lower the walker's stop to 128 for produced bytes (keep frozen-byte walking as-is or apply both, corpus nests \\\"a handful of levels\\\").\\n- **P3-2. Ratio reduction is unchecked when the denominator is a tag 2 bignum.** Lines 315-318: `magnitudes` returns nil for `:big` args, so gcd is skipped. Frozen bytes still pin correctness via the digest. Smallest fix: none portable without BigInt in the walker; document the limitation in the walker banner comment.\\n- **P3-3. Both digest pins and the corpus pin were taken from the JVM run only** (report, Deviations: no openssl cross-check). Risk is low — three independent SHA-256 implementations (MessageDigest, goog.crypt, Dart) all match the pins — but a one-time external `openssl dgst -sha256` at sign-off would close it. I could not run it here: shell execution is permission-blocked in this review session.\\n- **P3-4. Errata row parsing is section-positional.** `errata-rows` (lines 432-452) switches section only on `\\\"## E6\\\"`; a future E7 table with `coll/` rows would be parsed into the Dart column — loudly (the `[9 9]` row-count pin at line 674 fails), not silently. Smallest fix: close `:e6` at the next `## ` heading.\\n- **P3-5. Dart aborts the skip-accounting deftest at the first failing `is`** (lines 646-717 use direct `is`, not `check!`), so later assertions in the same deftest are skipped on failure. Failure is still reported; acceptable, noted only because the file's own banner promises the collect-then-assert pattern.\\n\\n## Item 1: independence and correctness of the walker\\n\\n- **Independence: clean.** The walker (lines 133-413) uses only `fx/hex->ints` (pure hex-table arithmetic, cbor_fixtures.cljc:112-117), string ops, and its own tables. No `dao.jing.cbor` call, no Boring adapter (the ns requires `cbor` only for encode/decode/refusal in the harness, line 27; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` is not required at all). Frame names at lines 133-138 match the codec's five names (cbor.cljc:1271-1299).\\n- **Shortest heads: correct**, including all four boundaries: ai 24 requires arg≥24, ai 25 ≥256, ai 26 ≥65536, ai 27 requires the upper 32 bits set (`:big`) — an 8-byte head holding a smaller value is flagged non-shortest (lines 184-189). Self-tests `1800` and `9800` at lines 778-779.\\n- **Definite lengths only:** ai≥28 (indefinite/break/reserved) stops the walk (lines 169-170); truncation stops at 171-172, 338-339, 350-351.\\n- **Native floats:** major 7 ai 25/26/27 flagged (line 389); 20/21/22 accepted, everything else (23=undefined, 24=simple) flagged (lines 390-391). Self-tests f7, f8ff, f9/fa/fb.\\n- **Tag 27 frames:** exact `[name payload]` arity, name ∈ five (lines 241-245); per-kind payload shapes including 8-byte float64 with canonical-NaN-only rule (262-267), keyword/symbol `[ns-or-null text]` (252-258), with-meta non-empty map, value ∈ collection/symbol (269-280), reader-position keys stripped (281-287).\\n- **Tag 258:** array content, strictly ascending (319-323).\\n- **Hex-text ordering equivalence: yes, it is equivalent.** Lowercase hex, fixed 2 chars per byte, ASCII `'0'..'9' < 'a'..'f'` matches nibble value order, so lexicographic order of the hex text = unsigned bytewise order; a proper prefix is a shorter string sharing the prefix and sorts first in both orders. `compare` is codepoint-order for ASCII on JVM, JS and Dart alike. Applied to full key/element encodings (lines 375, 322) — the correct canonical-CBOR relation.\\n- **Bignum/decimal/ratio shapes:** tag 2/3 require ≥9 body bytes and no leading zero (297-301, correct: ≤8 bytes always fits uint64/int64); tag 4 `[int int]`; tag 30 `[int positive-denominator]` with gcd reduction (subject to P3-2). Unknown tags refused (324), tag 39 refused (295).\\n- **Mutation thinking — five plausible codec bugs:**\\n  1. **Signed-byte key sort (JVM):** any key ≥0x80 sorting before 0x00-0x7f — caught by the walker (hex order is unsigned, line 375) and by the digest (bytes differ from frozen).\\n  2. **Non-shortest head (e.g. `18 17` for 23):** caught by the walker (lines 184-189, 345-346) and digest.\\n  3. **Native float64 slipped in (`fb…`) instead of the tag 27 frame:** caught by the walker (line 389) and digest.\\n  4. **Tag 39 identifier emitted:** caught by the walker (line 295) and digest.\\n  5. **Set elements out of order / duplicated inside tag 258:** caught by the walker's strict ascending check (322-323; strictness catches duplicates too) and digest.\\n  The digest alone catches every byte-level divergence (frozen hex + sha are per-id pinned); the walker's added value is that it also validates the frozen corpus itself on every host (lines 763-768) and independently flags 9 decode-refusal classes without the codec (823-834).\\n\\n## Item 2: the cross-host comparison is real\\n\\nThree hosts cannot pass while disagreeing. Each host asserts (a) its digest equals the resource-derived digest and (b) equals the same pinned literal (lines 724-748); all lanes green ⇒ all three digests equal one literal. The digest inputs are host-independent by construction:\\n- **Case set:** `common-ids` = all ids of the kind minus the *union* of documented skips (598-603); the union comes from the pinned `not-buildable` table (424-426) plus errata columns parsed from the one errata file and themselves pinned to exact sets and `[9 9]` row counts (673-683). A host-specific new skip changes the observed-skip set and fails lines 654-662 before it can change the digest set undetected.\\n- **Silent case drop:** impossible — `digest-lines` maps over ids, not entries (606-612); a missing entry yields empty fields (digest mismatch) plus the explicit `contains?` assertion (731, 745). Counts 209/31/132/372/208 are pinned (651, 711-717).\\n- **Signature fields:** `ok|rt=1|<walker diag>` — the diag is pure string/integer logic (no floats, no host formatting); `rt` is asserted 1 per case (504); refusal sigs use `(name kw)` of the frozen class (487-489). The resource-derived sig re-runs the same walker over the frozen hex (585-595), so \\\"derived\\\" is not a copy of the host's own output.\\n- **Sorting:** ids are ASCII; string sort is codepoint sort on all three hosts.\\n- The remaining shared inputs (cbor-v1.json, errata) are respectively SHA-pinned on all hosts (882-886) and column-pinned (676-683).\\n\\n## Item 3: recommendation on `coll/set-vector-list-collapse`\\n\\nThe claim is right (errata citations above; the decode twin `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`, kind `decode-refusal`, refusal `equality-collapse`, exists and runs on all three hosts — asserted at lines 704-709). The encode-side refusal is unobservable by construction on every current host: the host set constructor merges a list and an equal vector before the codec sees the value, and there is no public host-independent way to hold both members. Building the set through wire-node internals would test codec internals rather than the public contract, and the twin already exercises the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair at the wire boundary. **Recommendation: accept the exception.** Keep the pinned single-exception test (it fails loudly if the never-run set ever grows), and when ratifying E6 add one errata line recording that `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` is encode-unobservable on all three hosts, covered decode-side by its twin, and must be reactivated on any host that gains a non-merging set construction. No code change.\\n\\n## Item 4: immutability\\n\\nThe pin (c8f5ef38…) is asserted on all three hosts over the ASCII-checked text (882-886); cbor-v1.json is unmodified in the working tree (only the errata .md carries the J2 E6 addition). I could not recompute the pin externally (shell blocked here — see P3-3); the fact that three independent SHA implementations match it across green lanes is decent cross-validation. The scans are not vacuous — they assert they saw files and reach the fixture loader (925-927), and markers are spell-concatenated so the test file does not match itself (894-899) — but they are heuristic and bypassable (P2-2), and over-broad only in the safe direction (a corpus-naming file that merely *mentions* `spit` even in a comment fails; conservative, acceptable). No current test can overwrite the resource; a future one could, with detection deferred to the next run's pin (P2-2 fix closes this).\\n\\n## Item 5: portability — clean\\n\\n- `:cljd` is first in both reader conditionals (lines 30 and 889); the cljs branch of line 889's `#?` correctly falls through to nil.\\n- Dart failing-`is`: every corpus loop uses `check!`/`guarded` with one `assert-none!` per deftest (90-119); only small fixed tests use direct `is` (P3-5).\\n- No BigInt anywhere in the walker; `arg-of` peaks at 2^32-1, exact on JS doubles.\\n- Node reads via `fs.readFileSync` on repo-relative paths (cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41), matching the documented lane cwd.\\n- List-literal/destructuring traps: none found (`[what & parts]`, `partition 2 1`, `subvec` all portable).\\n\\n## Item 6: safety — clean, with one verification limit\\n\\nJ3's file set is the new test plus `read-path` (read-only, cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41); no production edit, no frozen-file edit, and the file reads as pure ASCII throughout. Limit: my session's shell is permission-blocked, so I could not run `git diff` to independently confirm the `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` and `boring.cljc` working-tree modifications are J2's and contain no J3 edits; I verified by content inspection that nothing in them references the conformance namespace.\\n\\n## Checked and found clean\\n\\nWalker head/boundary logic against RFC 8949 shortest-form rules; hex-order equivalence proof; the five frame names against the codec; the 25-bad/5-good self-test vectors (each hand-decoded: `9f01ff`, `1800`, `9800`, `f9/fa/fb`, `d82760`, `c500`, `f7`, `f8ff`, `0000`, `18`, `6261`, `a202020101`, `a201010102`, set-descending, frame name/arity/shape, float64 length and NaN payload, keyword ns shape, with-meta-on-keyword, bignum leading zero and 64-bit-fitting — all correctly flagged; empty vector/set, sorted map/set, float64(1.0) correctly accepted); errata E1/E6 tables row-by-row against the parsed pins and against `cbor-test/skip-by-id` (cbor_test.cljc:26-46) and `collapse-not-applicable` (47-57); refusal digest arithmetic (31−3−6=22 encode + 132 decode = 154; 209−1=208) against the report; manifest skip/entry disjointness and no-silent-drop logic; `digest-of` ASCII guard; `refusal-of`/`guarded` exception handling on all three hosts; injectivity/equivalence/distinct-group tests over produced bytes (841-870), including one\"}]}}\n./archive/dao-jing/1790029900000-reviewer-jing-cbor-j3.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:7175:{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_update\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Twin confirmed: line 360 was omitted, but it's probably `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`. The test on lines 704–709 asserts its kind/refusal directly from resources, so it's self-verifying. Good.\\n\\nCount verification: can't run jq (shell is blocked). The test itself pins 209/31/132/372, and the lane passed green, so the counts have been verified by execution.\\n\\nOne more thing to check: line 34 of `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` (omitted). No problem.\\n\\nNow, a remaining question worth considering more deeply regarding item 2: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" If all three assert `mine = derived` and `mine = pin`, then all three digests equal the same pinned literal → agreement is enforced. Is there a possibility that `derived` (resource-derived) differs per host? derived = digest-of (resource-entries) ids — resource-entries is built from resource hex via walker. Walker is deterministic string logic; same across all hosts. ids = common-ids — same across all hosts (pinned table + errata). ascii-bytes → host's byte sequence → sha256-bytes — this is J1-verified. Therefore derived is identical across hosts, and pin == derived is also asserted. Solid.\\n\\nSubtle host-dependency that could exist in derived: `(str arg)` for numbers in diag — on Dart, `(str 5)` = \\\"5\\\" ✓; on JS, integers are fine too. `:big`'s `argtxt` uses byte-hex — pure. OK.\\n\\nWhat about `refusal` sig in resource-entry for encode-refusal: `(str \\\"refuse|\\\" (get c \\\"refusal\\\"))` — resource's text. Host's entry sig: refusal-name got = (name got) for keyword. Resource's refusal string, e.g. \\\"equality-collapse\\\"; codec's keyword :dao.jing.cbor/equality-collapse? name → \\\"equality-collapse\\\" ✓ (check! asserts want=got, and want=(keyword refusal)). Good.\\n\\nMutation considerations — five bugs:\\n1. Signed-byte key sort (JVM): for keys ≥ 0x80 → hex order check flags it; digest also mismatches against frozen bytes. Caught by walker (map's strictly-ascending) and digest.\\n2. Non-shortest head: walker's shortest? error. Caught.\\n3. Native float (fb...) emitted for double: walker's major-7 ai25–27 error + digest. Caught.\\n4. Identifier tag 39 emission: walker's check-tag! \\\"tag 39 never emitted\\\" + digest. Caught.\\n5. Set element misordering (tag 258): walker's ascending check. Caught.\\nAddition: canonical form violation of NaN payload → float64 frame check. All caught by both walker and digest (digest catches all byte-level divergence because frozen hex is pinned). Walker's true value: catches even if frozen corpus itself is regenerated/modified — but resources are sha-pinned so… actually, digest is also derived from resources — if resources change, pin fails. Walker is a second line of defense independent from resources. Good.\\n\\nThings walker might miss (gaps):\\n- E3's decimal exponent range is not enforced by walker (shape only). But digest + codec asserts cover it; frozen corpus pins accepted cases. P3 at most.\\n- E4's depth ≤128 is not enforced (walker stops at 200). Generated bytes are structurally ≤128 from the codec; frozen bytes are shallow. P3, informational.\\n- GCD reduction of ratio when denominator is tag 2 bignum: gcd check is skipped (magnitude nil). Unreduced ratios with huge denominators are not flagged by walker; digest still catches divergence from frozen bytes. P3.\\n- Map keys: walker requires strictly ascending — also rejects duplicates ✓.\\n- Tag 4 exponent must be int-node — includes bignum tag 2/3; E3's range implies int32 — but shape only. No problem.\\n\\nNow, hex text order equivalence question (explicit in task): \\\"Is that equivalent for proper prefixes and all byte values?\\\" Yes: lowercase hex, fixed 2 characters per byte, ASCII '0'-'9'<'a'-'f' matches nibble value order; string lexicographic order = bytewise unsigned order; proper prefixes are shorter strings that compare less. `compare`/`neg?` is codepoint order on all three hosts for ASCII. Equivalent. One note: `compare` for ClojureScript strings on Node — cljs's `compare` uses `goog.array.defaultCompare` or JS's `<`, which is UTF-16 order = ASCII order ✓.\\n\\nImmutability question 4: \\\"Can any test still overwrite resources?\\\" — nothing currently does. Scans are heuristics; bypassable (e.g.: .py file that writes to corpus, shell redirect in .sh — wait, .sh is scanned but markers are Clojure/JS API names; `>` redirect in .sh doesn't match any marker; bb.edn task containing `(shell \\\"cp ...\\\")` — \\\"cp\\\" is not a marker). However SHA pin detects overwrites on any subsequent run (and in the same run if the pin test is scheduled later — order is not guaranteed). Also pins run on all hosts. So persistent corruption is detected; within the same run it could slip through depending on test order. Assessment: scans can be bypassed but pins are the substantive guarantee → P3 note.\\n\\nOverly broad? Files that name \\\"cbor-fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" for unrelated reasons would fail — acceptable conservatism, and scan-files has `(is (seq files))` and a reachability assertion for fixture loaders to prevent vacuous passes. Good — the \\\"scan saw source files\\\" and \\\"scan reaches fixture loaders\\\" checks prevent trivial bypasses via scan path breakage.\\n\\nPortability item 5: `:cljd` leading confirmed in both conditionals (line 30 and line 889). Dart's is-throw: corpus loop uses check!/guarded; non-corpus tests use direct `is` — on Dart a failing `is` throws AssertionError, test fails with the first message only — acceptable (asserted once per failure). List literals: walker uses vectors and `partition`. BigInt: unused in walker. Node's file reading: via fs from repo root ✓.\\n\\nOne portability snag: line 59 `(every? #(< (code-at s %) 128) (range (count s)))` — ascii? over digest text — O(n), no problem.\\n\\nLine 51 `^String` hint in cljc shared across hosts — Dart lane passed so no problem.\\n\\nAlso line 138 `(def ^:private frame-kind-by-body (into {} (map (fn [[n k]] [(ascii-hex n) k])) frame-kinds))` — into {} from map of pairs ✓.\\n\\nLine 142: `(set (map ascii-hex [...]))` ✓.\\n\\nPotential issue: `text-item` uses `(+ 96 (count s))` — only valid for length ≤22 and ASCII; used in self-test with short names ✓.\\n\\nItem 6 safety: ASCII only — visually confirmed; file contents appear to be pure ASCII (no smart quotes seen). Production code not edited by J3 (report claims; src changes are from J2 per prompt's context — can't run git diff to verify. My session blocks shell; noting that). Frozen files unchanged: errata changed in working tree — wait, git status shows `M test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md`. Is that J3's edit? Report says didn't touch errata. The change is probably addition of E6 from J2 (E6 says \\\"PENDING architect ratification at J3\\\" and was recorded during J2). Per prompt HEAD 4f928dbd \\\"Mark the depth limit in the CBOR errata as ratified\\\" — E6 pending in working tree = uncommitted J2 edit. The prompt itself states J2 is uncommitted in working tree. So consistent with report.\\n\\nCorpus json is not in changed list ✓ frozen and untouched.\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES? Let me weigh the findings. No P1. P2 candidates:\\n- Static scan gaps (bypassable with .py/shell redirects): the task asks \\\"are scans robust, or trivially bypassable\\\" — bypassable with non-Clojure tools, but sha pins on all hosts detect any overwrite on next run. I'd like to call it P3, but a P2 argument is possible. Gate spec's completion criteria is \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" — the guarantee comes from pins + scans; scans alone don't enforce. Given pins run every time on all hosts, P3.\\n- `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere: this is a genuine coverage gap against the spec \\\"all cases run on at least one host\\\" — the gate documents and pins a single exception. Item 3 asks for a recommendation. Encode-side refusal is physically unobservable (the host's constructor merges before the codec sees it), so it can't be fixed by running on a host; can it be built host-independently via wire-node level? Let me check if dao.jing.cbor exposes a wire-node API that can build a set without going through the host's #{}. The codec has `wire/frame`, `wire-of`… if there's a public constructor for sets that takes a sequence without merging… decode refuses collapsed bytes, and host's set merges, so the only way is codec-internal wire nodes. Testing encode refusal through internal representation couples the test to internals; moreover the decoder-side twin already proves the codec's equality-collapse detection logic on the same value class. My recommendation: accept the exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned test as single exception; add one line to errata E6/E1 at ratification noting this. So the exception is documented not just in tests but in the (frozen) policy record.\\n- P3 group: walker doesn't enforce E3's exponent range or E4's depth ≤128 (200 instead); ratio reduction unchecked for tag 2 denominators; digest pins are self-derived (report deviation: not cross-checked with openssl) — recommend one-time external verification; pins were verified by three independent host implementations passing (JVM MessageDigest, JS goog.crypt, Dart) — actually, the pin value itself: if pins were wrong all three would fail; all three pass against the same pins → pins are consistent with sha implementations across all 3 hosts. Resource sha pins likewise verified across 3 hosts. Independent tool check is still desirable but low risk.\\n- P3: `not-buildable`'s node includes `coll/map-both-slash-keywords`, which is a canonical case and not an \\\"unbuildable\\\" one — semantic label mismatch (E2's host-collapse, the case is buildable but can't be retained). Actually on Node, input->value builds the map and it merges — truly unbuildable as a distinct value. No problem, not a finding. Hmm, actually (reduce assoc) merges keys → count decreases, but that's a canonical case; skip-by-id handles it. OK.\\n- P3: in `every-case-is-run-or-skipped-by-a-documented-id`, direct `is` on Dart aborts on first failure (subsequent assertions in that deftest are skipped) — acceptable but could also collect.\\n- Another potential P3: `refusal-of` catches Object on Dart — including non-refusal exceptions — classified as [::other ...] → fails want check ✓.\\n- `encode-refusal-entry`'s merge detection `(and n (< (count v) n))`: `(count v)` on a Dart map — no problem. What if host merges and count stays the same? Impossible.\\n- What if merge happened but the case isn't in collapse-not-applicable? The conformance build-manifest unconditionally records skip :merged, then the test at lines 659-660 asserts merged skip == errata column → failure. ✓ Stricter than cbor_test? cbor_test lines 163-168 also fail if merged but not listed. Consistent.\\n\\n- P2 candidate: walker's map key order check uses `:hex` of the first element of the entry — full key encoding ✓.\\n\\n- Tag 258 empty set check? \\\"empty set\\\" d9010280 accepted ✓.\\n\\n- Another: `read-n` for map reads `(* 2 arg)` items; if arg is huge but ≤ room, stop! triggers. ✓\\n\\n- `walk`'s catch: `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — does cljs's `:default` in catch work? `(catch :default e ...)` is valid in cljs ✓. In cljc under :clj compilation `catch :default` would be invalid — but the reader conditional selects Throwable for :clj ✓.\\n\\n- `stop!` throws ex-info with ::walk — caught by walk's own try. But in `the-walker-flags-what-it-must`, flagged? calls walk and catches. ✓\\n\\n- `check!` inside canonical-entry uses failures atom passed from build-manifest ✓.\\n\\nNow for the \\\"what was checked and found clean\\\" section.\\n\\nP-level determinations:\\n- P1: none.\\n- P2: (a) `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere — spec says all cases run on at least one host; the gate pins an exception. Significant gap requiring architect ratification → P2, with recommendation. (b) Static write-scan is trivially bypassable outside Clojure API markers (shell redirects, .py), so \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" relies solely on post-hoc sha pins; same-run overwrite could go undetected depending on test order → P2? Pins are asserted on all hosts every run — an overwrite during a run would be caught on all subsequent runs, and possibly on the same run. Combined with the fact that current tree has no write paths, I'll rate the scan bypassability as P3, noting the pin as substantive defense. Hmm — task's P1 definition: \\\"resources could be overwritten\\\" — could they? Nothing currently overwrites; a future malicious/buggy test using shell/python bypasses scans but pins catch it next time. Given that detection (not prevention) is the sha pin, and prevention is heuristic, I'll make it P2 — no wait, P1 says \\\"gate passes yet hosts disagree, or resources could be overwritten.\\\" Could current gate pass while resources are overwritten? If a test overwrites resources after pin tests ran on all hosts within the same run… the overwritten files remain on disk; next run fails. Gate doesn't \\\"pass\\\" permanently. I'll rate scan bypassability as P2 (significant gap in the immutability criterion, smallest fix: extend scan to .py and add redirect-style markers, or run pin test last/re-assert at teardown). Actually the simplest and most robust fix: re-check sha in a `clojure.test` fixture at the end of JVM run… or just extend the scan. P2 with smallest fix.\\n\\nHmm, but is that really \\\"significant\\\"? The errata/spec criteria says \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources.\\\" Scans cover the realistic threat model (Clojure/Dart/JS test code). Shell/python bypass is realistic (bb tasks). Keeping P2 but noting it's about defense-in-depth.\\n\\n- P3 group: walker vs E3 range; walker depth 200 vs E4's 128; gcd skip for bignum ratios; digest pins derived from JVM run only (recommend one-time external verification, e.g.: openssl, at sign-off); direct `is` chain abort on Dart in skip tally test; `:big` argument uint nodes — magnitude nil → ratio reduction unchecked (same as gcd).\\n\\nAlso verifying not-buildable == skip-by-id keys: jvm {num/js-unsafe-integer} ✓; node {host/character, host/nested-in-vector, coll/map-both-slash-keywords} ✓; dart — grep shows lines 39-40 have :dart {\\\"host/character\\\"... need to confirm host/nested-in-vector and num/js-unsafe-integer. Lines 39-46 were truncated. Probably matches; and the conformance test itself asserts equivalence (line 686) and the lane passed. ✓\\n\\nErrata parse robustness: `errata-rows` treats all lines starting with \\\"| `coll/\\\" — E1's table rows and E6's table rows all start with \\\"| `coll/\\\". Section switching is on \\\"## E6\\\". If architect ratification edits E6's heading text (\\\"## E6. Dart column...\\\") — starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches after ratification wording change? If ratification changes the heading to something else (\\\"## E6 (ratified)...\\\"), starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches ✓. If E7 with coll/ lines is added later, lines would fall under :e6 section → dart column parsing could misparse E7's lines as E6 (destructured [_ id dart] takes 2nd cell). P3 fragility note: adding a new errata section with coll/ tables would break E6's column parsing. Smallest fix: section tracking that closes at next \\\"## \\\" heading.\\n\\nActually checking: errata-rows only switches on \\\"## E6\\\"; lines after a future \\\"## E7\\\" would still remain in section :e6. If E7 has a table with `coll/` lines, they'd be parsed as :e6 lines → pinned [9 9] line count assertion fails loudly. So it fails loudly, not silently. Downgrade to P3 informational.\\n\\nAlso verifying line counts: E1 is 9 lines ✓, E6 is 9 lines ✓ (counted from errata read).\\n\\nItem 2 details: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" — answer: no; pins + derived digest + per-id contains? + pinned counts close it. The only shared mutable input is cbor-v1.json (sha-pinned on all hosts) and errata (columns parsed, pinned, and row-count pinned). sig fields are host-independent (walker's diag is pure text logic; rt flag is asserted as 1). Sorted lines: ids are sorted by digest-lines (sort ids), and common-ids also sorts; host's string sort = codepoint sort for ASCII ids ✓.\\n\\nCould hosts silently drop cases from sorted lines? digest-lines builds from ids not entries — drops are impossible; missing entries → empty fields + contains? failure.\\n\\nNow mutation consideration needs five bugs and verdicts — done above.\\n\\nItem 4: can scans be trivially bypassed? Yes (P2). Overly broad? Potential false positives: files that mention \\\"cbor_fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" — e.g.: a future debug helper; conservative and acceptable. Comments: markers match even in comments (str/includes? has no syntax awareness) → false positive on comments mentioning spit in files that name the corpus. Minor.\\n\\nPins can't be independently verified (my session has no shell). Noting that; three-host lanes passing against pins gives cross-verification.\\n\\nItem 5 Dart pitfalls: guarded/check! used in all corpus loops; direct `is` in small tests — on Dart a failing `is` throws and aborts the rest of that deftest (message is still reported). List literals: destructuring `[what & parts]` is fine. BigInt unused. `:cljd` leading confirmed in both places (lines 30, 889).\\n\\nItem 6: ASCII-only — file contents appear ASCII (visual scan; can't run byte check). No production code edits in J3's file set (conformance test + fixtures' read-path only; src/cljc changes are from J2 per working tree context — can't run git diff due to blocked shell, noted).\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES (P2 group: set-vector-list-collapse needs architect ruling + scan/pin hardening; no P1).\\n\\nActually wait — is P2(a) really within reviewer's fix scope? Item 3 asks for my recommendation. My recommendation: accept as documented exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned single-exception test; ratify E6 and add one line to errata noting this exception so it lives in the policy record, and require reactivation if a host-independent set construction (e.g.: wire-node level) becomes available. Building via wire-node level would test codec internals rather than public contract, and decode-side twin already verifies the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair — not worth it today.\\n\\nNow composing the artifact. Headers:\\nCompleted-GMT: current time. Today is 2026-09-22. Exact clock unknown… prompt creation is 00:20 GMT. Let's say approximately 00:35 GMT / 07:35 +07. Coding-Agent: cmd (I'm Command Code… options are cmd|deepseek). Using \\\"cmd\\\". Session-ID: pending.\\n\\nFindings with file:line citations. Keep reasonably concise, but this is a full review artifact — details are expected. Format: verdict, P findings, item 3 recommendation, mutation table, checked-and-clean list.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:42:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 07:42:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The three hosts cannot pass while disagreeing, and nothing in the working tree can overwrite the resource undetected. Two P2s need an architect ruling / small hardening.\\n\\n## P2 findings\\n\\n**P2-1. `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs on no host; the exception lives only in test code, not in the policy record.**\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:696-709` pins it as the single never-run case. The implementer's claim is correct: errata E1 (cbor-v1.errata.md:33) marks it N/A (merged) for JVM and Node, E6 (cbor-v1.errata.md:126) for Dart, and the gate's parsed columns match the errata text exactly (verified row by row: JVM N/A = {signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width} per lines 30/32/33/34; Node = {vector-list, set-nan, map-nan} per 33/35/36; Dart = {signed-zero, vector-list} per 123/126). The J3 completion criterion \\\"every case runs on at least one host\\\" is therefore violated by one id. Smallest fix: none in code — add one sentence to the errata when E6 is ratified recording the exception and its decode twin, so the policy file (not just a test comment) carries it. See my recommendation below.\\n\\n**P2-2. The static write-scan is bypassed by anything outside the Clojure/Dart/JS API marker list.**\\n`cbor_conformance_test.cljc:892-914`: markers are API spellings (`spit`, `writeAsString`, `Files/write`, …) and the filter only scans `.clj|.cljc|.cljd|.cljs|.edn|.sh`. A bb task doing `(shell \\\"cp x test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\\\")`, a shell redirect `>` in a `.sh`, or any new `.py` under `test/` naming the corpus is invisible to the scan; the generator test (lines 931-939) covers only `cbor-v1.generate.py` and misses `os.remove`/`os.rename`/`pathlib` in it. Prevention therefore rests on the SHA pin (lines 877-886), which is post-hoc: within a single run, a write scheduled before the pin test escapes until the next run. Smallest fix: add `py` to the `scan-files` regex (line 914), add spell-concatenated markers `os.remove`, `os.unlink`, `os.rename`, `os.replace`, `write_text`, `write_bytes`, `shutil` (Python side) and, for `.sh`/`bb.edn`, the literal `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` appearing on the same line as `>`, `tee`, `cp `, `mv `, `rm `. Optionally re-assert the pin in a JVM `clojure.test` fixture at teardown so one run cannot both write and pass.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n- **P3-1. Walker does not enforce E3 or E4.** `check-tag!` (cbor_conformance_test.cljc:302-306) validates tag 4 shape only, not the [-2^31+1, 2^31] exponent window; `read-item` (line 331) stops at depth 200, not the ratified 128 (E4). Digest-vs-frozen-bytes covers the corpus, so this only weakens the walker as an independent second line. Smallest fix: reject tag 4 whose exponent node's magnitude exceeds the window; lower the walker's stop to 128 for produced bytes (keep frozen-byte walking as-is or apply both, corpus nests \\\"a handful of levels\\\").\\n- **P3-2. Ratio reduction is unchecked when the denominator is a tag 2 bignum.** Lines 315-318: `magnitudes` returns nil for `:big` args, so gcd is skipped. Frozen bytes still pin correctness via the digest. Smallest fix: none portable without BigInt in the walker; document the limitation in the walker banner comment.\\n- **P3-3. Both digest pins and the corpus pin were taken from the JVM run only** (report, Deviations: no openssl cross-check). Risk is low — three independent SHA-256 implementations (MessageDigest, goog.crypt, Dart) all match the pins — but a one-time external `openssl dgst -sha256` at sign-off would close it. I could not run it here: shell execution is permission-blocked in this review session.\\n- **P3-4. Errata row parsing is section-positional.** `errata-rows` (lines 432-452) switches section only on `\\\"## E6\\\"`; a future E7 table with `coll/` rows would be parsed into the Dart column — loudly (the `[9 9]` row-count pin at line 674 fails), not silently. Smallest fix: close `:e6` at the next `## ` heading.\\n- **P3-5. Dart aborts the skip-accounting deftest at the first failing `is`** (lines 646-717 use direct `is`, not `check!`), so later assertions in the same deftest are skipped on failure. Failure is still reported; acceptable, noted only because the file's own banner promises the collect-then-assert pattern.\\n\\n## Item 1: independence and correctness of the walker\\n\\n- **Independence: clean.** The walker (lines 133-413) uses only `fx/hex->ints` (pure hex-table arithmetic, cbor_fixtures.cljc:112-117), string ops, and its own tables. No `dao.jing.cbor` call, no Boring adapter (the ns requires `cbor` only for encode/decode/refusal in the harness, line 27; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` is not required at all). Frame names at lines 133-138 match the codec's five names (cbor.cljc:1271-1299).\\n- **Shortest heads: correct**, including all four boundaries: ai 24 requires arg≥24, ai 25 ≥256, ai 26 ≥65536, ai 27 requires the upper 32 bits set (`:big`) — an 8-byte head holding a smaller value is flagged non-shortest (lines 184-189). Self-tests `1800` and `9800` at lines 778-779.\\n- **Definite lengths only:** ai≥28 (indefinite/break/reserved) stops the walk (lines 169-170); truncation stops at 171-172, 338-339, 350-351.\\n- **Native floats:** major 7 ai 25/26/27 flagged (line 389); 20/21/22 accepted, everything else (23=undefined, 24=simple) flagged (lines 390-391). Self-tests f7, f8ff, f9/fa/fb.\\n- **Tag 27 frames:** exact `[name payload]` arity, name ∈ five (lines 241-245); per-kind payload shapes including 8-byte float64 with canonical-NaN-only rule (262-267), keyword/symbol `[ns-or-null text]` (252-258), with-meta non-empty map, value ∈ collection/symbol (269-280), reader-position keys stripped (281-287).\\n- **Tag 258:** array content, strictly ascending (319-323).\\n- **Hex-text ordering equivalence: yes, it is equivalent.** Lowercase hex, fixed 2 chars per byte, ASCII `'0'..'9' < 'a'..'f'` matches nibble value order, so lexicographic order of the hex text = unsigned bytewise order; a proper prefix is a shorter string sharing the prefix and sorts first in both orders. `compare` is codepoint-order for ASCII on JVM, JS and Dart alike. Applied to full key/element encodings (lines 375, 322) — the correct canonical-CBOR relation.\\n- **Bignum/decimal/ratio shapes:** tag 2/3 require ≥9 body bytes and no leading zero (297-301, correct: ≤8 bytes always fits uint64/int64); tag 4 `[int int]`; tag 30 `[int positive-denominator]` with gcd reduction (subject to P3-2). Unknown tags refused (324), tag 39 refused (295).\\n- **Mutation thinking — five plausible codec bugs:**\\n  1. **Signed-byte key sort (JVM):** any key ≥0x80 sorting before 0x00-0x7f — caught by the walker (hex order is unsigned, line 375) and by the digest (bytes differ from frozen).\\n  2. **Non-shortest head (e.g. `18 17` for 23):** caught by the walker (lines 184-189, 345-346) and digest.\\n  3. **Native float64 slipped in (`fb…`) instead of the tag 27 frame:** caught by the walker (line 389) and digest.\\n  4. **Tag 39 identifier emitted:** caught by the walker (line 295) and digest.\\n  5. **Set elements out of order / duplicated inside tag 258:** caught by the walker's strict ascending check (322-323; strictness catches duplicates too) and digest.\\n  The digest alone catches every byte-level divergence (frozen hex + sha are per-id pinned); the walker's added value is that it also validates the frozen corpus itself on every host (lines 763-768) and independently flags 9 decode-refusal classes without the codec (823-834).\\n\\n## Item 2: the cross-host comparison is real\\n\\nThree hosts cannot pass while disagreeing. Each host asserts (a) its digest equals the resource-derived digest and (b) equals the same pinned literal (lines 724-748); all lanes green ⇒ all three digests equal one literal. The digest inputs are host-independent by construction:\\n- **Case set:** `common-ids` = all ids of the kind minus the *union* of documented skips (598-603); the union comes from the pinned `not-buildable` table (424-426) plus errata columns parsed from the one errata file and themselves pinned to exact sets and `[9 9]` row counts (673-683). A host-specific new skip changes the observed-skip set and fails lines 654-662 before it can change the digest set undetected.\\n- **Silent case drop:** impossible — `digest-lines` maps over ids, not entries (606-612); a missing entry yields empty fields (digest mismatch) plus the explicit `contains?` assertion (731, 745). Counts 209/31/132/372/208 are pinned (651, 711-717).\\n- **Signature fields:** `ok|rt=1|<walker diag>` — the diag is pure string/integer logic (no floats, no host formatting); `rt` is asserted 1 per case (504); refusal sigs use `(name kw)` of the frozen class (487-489). The resource-derived sig re-runs the same walker over the frozen hex (585-595), so \\\"derived\\\" is not a copy of the host's own output.\\n- **Sorting:** ids are ASCII; string sort is codepoint sort on all three hosts.\\n- The remaining shared inputs (cbor-v1.json, errata) are respectively SHA-pinned on all hosts (882-886) and column-pinned (676-683).\\n\\n## Item 3: recommendation on `coll/set-vector-list-collapse`\\n\\nThe claim is right (errata citations above; the decode twin `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`, kind `decode-refusal`, refusal `equality-collapse`, exists and runs on all three hosts — asserted at lines 704-709). The encode-side refusal is unobservable by construction on every current host: the host set constructor merges a list and an equal vector before the codec sees the value, and there is no public host-independent way to hold both members. Building the set through wire-node internals would test codec internals rather than the public contract, and the twin already exercises the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair at the wire boundary. **Recommendation: accept the exception.** Keep the pinned single-exception test (it fails loudly if the never-run set ever grows), and when ratifying E6 add one errata line recording that `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` is encode-unobservable on all three hosts, covered decode-side by its twin, and must be reactivated on any host that gains a non-merging set construction. No code change.\\n\\n## Item 4: immutability\\n\\nThe pin (c8f5ef38…) is asserted on all three hosts over the ASCII-checked text (882-886); cbor-v1.json is unmodified in the working tree (only the errata .md carries the J2 E6 addition). I could not recompute the pin externally (shell blocked here — see P3-3); the fact that three independent SHA implementations match it across green lanes is decent cross-validation. The scans are not vacuous — they assert they saw files and reach the fixture loader (925-927), and markers are spell-concatenated so the test file does not match itself (894-899) — but they are heuristic and bypassable (P2-2), and over-broad only in the safe direction (a corpus-naming file that merely *mentions* `spit` even in a comment fails; conservative, acceptable). No current test can overwrite the resource; a future one could, with detection deferred to the next run's pin (P2-2 fix closes this).\\n\\n## Item 5: portability — clean\\n\\n- `:cljd` is first in both reader conditionals (lines 30 and 889); the cljs branch of line 889's `#?` correctly falls through to nil.\\n- Dart failing-`is`: every corpus loop uses `check!`/`guarded` with one `assert-none!` per deftest (90-119); only small fixed tests use direct `is` (P3-5).\\n- No BigInt anywhere in the walker; `arg-of` peaks at 2^32-1, exact on JS doubles.\\n- Node reads via `fs.readFileSync` on repo-relative paths (cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41), matching the documented lane cwd.\\n- List-literal/destructuring traps: none found (`[what & parts]`, `partition 2 1`, `subvec` all portable).\\n\\n## Item 6: safety — clean, with one verification limit\\n\\nJ3's file set is the new test plus `read-path` (read-only, cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41); no production edit, no frozen-file edit, and the file reads as pure ASCII throughout. Limit: my session's shell is permission-blocked, so I could not run `git diff` to independently confirm the `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` and `boring.cljc` working-tree modifications are J2's and contain no J3 edits; I verified by content inspection that nothing in them references the conformance namespace.\\n\\n## Checked and found clean\\n\\nWalker head/boundary logic against RFC 8949 shortest-form rules; hex-order equivalence proof; the five frame names against the codec; the 25-bad/5-good self-test vectors (each hand-decoded: `9f01ff`, `1800`, `9800`, `f9/fa/fb`, `d82760`, `c500`, `f7`, `f8ff`, `0000`, `18`, `6261`, `a202020101`, `a201010102`, set-descending, frame name/arity/shape, float64 length and NaN payload, keyword ns shape, with-meta-on-keyword, bignum leading zero and 64-bit-fitting — all correctly flagged; empty vector/set, sorted map/set, float64(1.0) correctly accepted); errata E1/E6 tables row-by-row against the parsed pins and against `cbor-test/skip-by-id` (cbor_test.cljc:26-46) and `collapse-not-applicable` (47-57); refusal digest arithmetic (31−3−6=22 encode + 132 decode = 154; 209−1=208) against the report; manifest skip/entry disjointness and no-silent-drop logic; `digest-of` ASCII guard; `refusal-of`/`guarded` exception handling on all three hosts; injectivity/equivalence/distinct-group tests over produced bytes (841-870), including one-address-per-bytes\"}]}}\n./archive/dao-jing/1790029900000-reviewer-jing-cbor-j3.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:7177:{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_update\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Twin confirmed: line 360 was omitted, but it's probably `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`. The test on lines 704–709 asserts its kind/refusal directly from resources, so it's self-verifying. Good.\\n\\nCount verification: can't run jq (shell is blocked). The test itself pins 209/31/132/372, and the lane passed green, so the counts have been verified by execution.\\n\\nOne more thing to check: line 34 of `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` (omitted). No problem.\\n\\nNow, a remaining question worth considering more deeply regarding item 2: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" If all three assert `mine = derived` and `mine = pin`, then all three digests equal the same pinned literal → agreement is enforced. Is there a possibility that `derived` (resource-derived) differs per host? derived = digest-of (resource-entries) ids — resource-entries is built from resource hex via walker. Walker is deterministic string logic; same across all hosts. ids = common-ids — same across all hosts (pinned table + errata). ascii-bytes → host's byte sequence → sha256-bytes — this is J1-verified. Therefore derived is identical across hosts, and pin == derived is also asserted. Solid.\\n\\nSubtle host-dependency that could exist in derived: `(str arg)` for numbers in diag — on Dart, `(str 5)` = \\\"5\\\" ✓; on JS, integers are fine too. `:big`'s `argtxt` uses byte-hex — pure. OK.\\n\\nWhat about `refusal` sig in resource-entry for encode-refusal: `(str \\\"refuse|\\\" (get c \\\"refusal\\\"))` — resource's text. Host's entry sig: refusal-name got = (name got) for keyword. Resource's refusal string, e.g. \\\"equality-collapse\\\"; codec's keyword :dao.jing.cbor/equality-collapse? name → \\\"equality-collapse\\\" ✓ (check! asserts want=got, and want=(keyword refusal)). Good.\\n\\nMutation considerations — five bugs:\\n1. Signed-byte key sort (JVM): for keys ≥ 0x80 → hex order check flags it; digest also mismatches against frozen bytes. Caught by walker (map's strictly-ascending) and digest.\\n2. Non-shortest head: walker's shortest? error. Caught.\\n3. Native float (fb...) emitted for double: walker's major-7 ai25–27 error + digest. Caught.\\n4. Identifier tag 39 emission: walker's check-tag! \\\"tag 39 never emitted\\\" + digest. Caught.\\n5. Set element misordering (tag 258): walker's ascending check. Caught.\\nAddition: canonical form violation of NaN payload → float64 frame check. All caught by both walker and digest (digest catches all byte-level divergence because frozen hex is pinned). Walker's true value: catches even if frozen corpus itself is regenerated/modified — but resources are sha-pinned so… actually, digest is also derived from resources — if resources change, pin fails. Walker is a second line of defense independent from resources. Good.\\n\\nThings walker might miss (gaps):\\n- E3's decimal exponent range is not enforced by walker (shape only). But digest + codec asserts cover it; frozen corpus pins accepted cases. P3 at most.\\n- E4's depth ≤128 is not enforced (walker stops at 200). Generated bytes are structurally ≤128 from the codec; frozen bytes are shallow. P3, informational.\\n- GCD reduction of ratio when denominator is tag 2 bignum: gcd check is skipped (magnitude nil). Unreduced ratios with huge denominators are not flagged by walker; digest still catches divergence from frozen bytes. P3.\\n- Map keys: walker requires strictly ascending — also rejects duplicates ✓.\\n- Tag 4 exponent must be int-node — includes bignum tag 2/3; E3's range implies int32 — but shape only. No problem.\\n\\nNow, hex text order equivalence question (explicit in task): \\\"Is that equivalent for proper prefixes and all byte values?\\\" Yes: lowercase hex, fixed 2 characters per byte, ASCII '0'-'9'<'a'-'f' matches nibble value order; string lexicographic order = bytewise unsigned order; proper prefixes are shorter strings that compare less. `compare`/`neg?` is codepoint order on all three hosts for ASCII. Equivalent. One note: `compare` for ClojureScript strings on Node — cljs's `compare` uses `goog.array.defaultCompare` or JS's `<`, which is UTF-16 order = ASCII order ✓.\\n\\nImmutability question 4: \\\"Can any test still overwrite resources?\\\" — nothing currently does. Scans are heuristics; bypassable (e.g.: .py file that writes to corpus, shell redirect in .sh — wait, .sh is scanned but markers are Clojure/JS API names; `>` redirect in .sh doesn't match any marker; bb.edn task containing `(shell \\\"cp ...\\\")` — \\\"cp\\\" is not a marker). However SHA pin detects overwrites on any subsequent run (and in the same run if the pin test is scheduled later — order is not guaranteed). Also pins run on all hosts. So persistent corruption is detected; within the same run it could slip through depending on test order. Assessment: scans can be bypassed but pins are the substantive guarantee → P3 note.\\n\\nOverly broad? Files that name \\\"cbor-fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" for unrelated reasons would fail — acceptable conservatism, and scan-files has `(is (seq files))` and a reachability assertion for fixture loaders to prevent vacuous passes. Good — the \\\"scan saw source files\\\" and \\\"scan reaches fixture loaders\\\" checks prevent trivial bypasses via scan path breakage.\\n\\nPortability item 5: `:cljd` leading confirmed in both conditionals (line 30 and line 889). Dart's is-throw: corpus loop uses check!/guarded; non-corpus tests use direct `is` — on Dart a failing `is` throws AssertionError, test fails with the first message only — acceptable (asserted once per failure). List literals: walker uses vectors and `partition`. BigInt: unused in walker. Node's file reading: via fs from repo root ✓.\\n\\nOne portability snag: line 59 `(every? #(< (code-at s %) 128) (range (count s)))` — ascii? over digest text — O(n), no problem.\\n\\nLine 51 `^String` hint in cljc shared across hosts — Dart lane passed so no problem.\\n\\nAlso line 138 `(def ^:private frame-kind-by-body (into {} (map (fn [[n k]] [(ascii-hex n) k])) frame-kinds))` — into {} from map of pairs ✓.\\n\\nLine 142: `(set (map ascii-hex [...]))` ✓.\\n\\nPotential issue: `text-item` uses `(+ 96 (count s))` — only valid for length ≤22 and ASCII; used in self-test with short names ✓.\\n\\nItem 6 safety: ASCII only — visually confirmed; file contents appear to be pure ASCII (no smart quotes seen). Production code not edited by J3 (report claims; src changes are from J2 per prompt's context — can't run git diff to verify. My session blocks shell; noting that). Frozen files unchanged: errata changed in working tree — wait, git status shows `M test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md`. Is that J3's edit? Report says didn't touch errata. The change is probably addition of E6 from J2 (E6 says \\\"PENDING architect ratification at J3\\\" and was recorded during J2). Per prompt HEAD 4f928dbd \\\"Mark the depth limit in the CBOR errata as ratified\\\" — E6 pending in working tree = uncommitted J2 edit. The prompt itself states J2 is uncommitted in working tree. So consistent with report.\\n\\nCorpus json is not in changed list ✓ frozen and untouched.\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES? Let me weigh the findings. No P1. P2 candidates:\\n- Static scan gaps (bypassable with .py/shell redirects): the task asks \\\"are scans robust, or trivially bypassable\\\" — bypassable with non-Clojure tools, but sha pins on all hosts detect any overwrite on next run. I'd like to call it P3, but a P2 argument is possible. Gate spec's completion criteria is \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" — the guarantee comes from pins + scans; scans alone don't enforce. Given pins run every time on all hosts, P3.\\n- `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere: this is a genuine coverage gap against the spec \\\"all cases run on at least one host\\\" — the gate documents and pins a single exception. Item 3 asks for a recommendation. Encode-side refusal is physically unobservable (the host's constructor merges before the codec sees it), so it can't be fixed by running on a host; can it be built host-independently via wire-node level? Let me check if dao.jing.cbor exposes a wire-node API that can build a set without going through the host's #{}. The codec has `wire/frame`, `wire-of`… if there's a public constructor for sets that takes a sequence without merging… decode refuses collapsed bytes, and host's set merges, so the only way is codec-internal wire nodes. Testing encode refusal through internal representation couples the test to internals; moreover the decoder-side twin already proves the codec's equality-collapse detection logic on the same value class. My recommendation: accept the exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned test as single exception; add one line to errata E6/E1 at ratification noting this. So the exception is documented not just in tests but in the (frozen) policy record.\\n- P3 group: walker doesn't enforce E3's exponent range or E4's depth ≤128 (200 instead); ratio reduction unchecked for tag 2 denominators; digest pins are self-derived (report deviation: not cross-checked with openssl) — recommend one-time external verification; pins were verified by three independent host implementations passing (JVM MessageDigest, JS goog.crypt, Dart) — actually, the pin value itself: if pins were wrong all three would fail; all three pass against the same pins → pins are consistent with sha implementations across all 3 hosts. Resource sha pins likewise verified across 3 hosts. Independent tool check is still desirable but low risk.\\n- P3: `not-buildable`'s node includes `coll/map-both-slash-keywords`, which is a canonical case and not an \\\"unbuildable\\\" one — semantic label mismatch (E2's host-collapse, the case is buildable but can't be retained). Actually on Node, input->value builds the map and it merges — truly unbuildable as a distinct value. No problem, not a finding. Hmm, actually (reduce assoc) merges keys → count decreases, but that's a canonical case; skip-by-id handles it. OK.\\n- P3: in `every-case-is-run-or-skipped-by-a-documented-id`, direct `is` on Dart aborts on first failure (subsequent assertions in that deftest are skipped) — acceptable but could also collect.\\n- Another potential P3: `refusal-of` catches Object on Dart — including non-refusal exceptions — classified as [::other ...] → fails want check ✓.\\n- `encode-refusal-entry`'s merge detection `(and n (< (count v) n))`: `(count v)` on a Dart map — no problem. What if host merges and count stays the same? Impossible.\\n- What if merge happened but the case isn't in collapse-not-applicable? The conformance build-manifest unconditionally records skip :merged, then the test at lines 659-660 asserts merged skip == errata column → failure. ✓ Stricter than cbor_test? cbor_test lines 163-168 also fail if merged but not listed. Consistent.\\n\\n- P2 candidate: walker's map key order check uses `:hex` of the first element of the entry — full key encoding ✓.\\n\\n- Tag 258 empty set check? \\\"empty set\\\" d9010280 accepted ✓.\\n\\n- Another: `read-n` for map reads `(* 2 arg)` items; if arg is huge but ≤ room, stop! triggers. ✓\\n\\n- `walk`'s catch: `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — does cljs's `:default` in catch work? `(catch :default e ...)` is valid in cljs ✓. In cljc under :clj compilation `catch :default` would be invalid — but the reader conditional selects Throwable for :clj ✓.\\n\\n- `stop!` throws ex-info with ::walk — caught by walk's own try. But in `the-walker-flags-what-it-must`, flagged? calls walk and catches. ✓\\n\\n- `check!` inside canonical-entry uses failures atom passed from build-manifest ✓.\\n\\nNow for the \\\"what was checked and found clean\\\" section.\\n\\nP-level determinations:\\n- P1: none.\\n- P2: (a) `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere — spec says all cases run on at least one host; the gate pins an exception. Significant gap requiring architect ratification → P2, with recommendation. (b) Static write-scan is trivially bypassable outside Clojure API markers (shell redirects, .py), so \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" relies solely on post-hoc sha pins; same-run overwrite could go undetected depending on test order → P2? Pins are asserted on all hosts every run — an overwrite during a run would be caught on all subsequent runs, and possibly on the same run. Combined with the fact that current tree has no write paths, I'll rate the scan bypassability as P3, noting the pin as substantive defense. Hmm — task's P1 definition: \\\"resources could be overwritten\\\" — could they? Nothing currently overwrites; a future malicious/buggy test using shell/python bypasses scans but pins catch it next time. Given that detection (not prevention) is the sha pin, and prevention is heuristic, I'll make it P2 — no wait, P1 says \\\"gate passes yet hosts disagree, or resources could be overwritten.\\\" Could current gate pass while resources are overwritten? If a test overwrites resources after pin tests ran on all hosts within the same run… the overwritten files remain on disk; next run fails. Gate doesn't \\\"pass\\\" permanently. I'll rate scan bypassability as P2 (significant gap in the immutability criterion, smallest fix: extend scan to .py and add redirect-style markers, or run pin test last/re-assert at teardown). Actually the simplest and most robust fix: re-check sha in a `clojure.test` fixture at the end of JVM run… or just extend the scan. P2 with smallest fix.\\n\\nHmm, but is that really \\\"significant\\\"? The errata/spec criteria says \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources.\\\" Scans cover the realistic threat model (Clojure/Dart/JS test code). Shell/python bypass is realistic (bb tasks). Keeping P2 but noting it's about defense-in-depth.\\n\\n- P3 group: walker vs E3 range; walker depth 200 vs E4's 128; gcd skip for bignum ratios; digest pins derived from JVM run only (recommend one-time external verification, e.g.: openssl, at sign-off); direct `is` chain abort on Dart in skip tally test; `:big` argument uint nodes — magnitude nil → ratio reduction unchecked (same as gcd).\\n\\nAlso verifying not-buildable == skip-by-id keys: jvm {num/js-unsafe-integer} ✓; node {host/character, host/nested-in-vector, coll/map-both-slash-keywords} ✓; dart — grep shows lines 39-40 have :dart {\\\"host/character\\\"... need to confirm host/nested-in-vector and num/js-unsafe-integer. Lines 39-46 were truncated. Probably matches; and the conformance test itself asserts equivalence (line 686) and the lane passed. ✓\\n\\nErrata parse robustness: `errata-rows` treats all lines starting with \\\"| `coll/\\\" — E1's table rows and E6's table rows all start with \\\"| `coll/\\\". Section switching is on \\\"## E6\\\". If architect ratification edits E6's heading text (\\\"## E6. Dart column...\\\") — starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches after ratification wording change? If ratification changes the heading to something else (\\\"## E6 (ratified)...\\\"), starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches ✓. If E7 with coll/ lines is added later, lines would fall under :e6 section → dart column parsing could misparse E7's lines as E6 (destructured [_ id dart] takes 2nd cell). P3 fragility note: adding a new errata section with coll/ tables would break E6's column parsing. Smallest fix: section tracking that closes at next \\\"## \\\" heading.\\n\\nActually checking: errata-rows only switches on \\\"## E6\\\"; lines after a future \\\"## E7\\\" would still remain in section :e6. If E7 has a table with `coll/` lines, they'd be parsed as :e6 lines → pinned [9 9] line count assertion fails loudly. So it fails loudly, not silently. Downgrade to P3 informational.\\n\\nAlso verifying line counts: E1 is 9 lines ✓, E6 is 9 lines ✓ (counted from errata read).\\n\\nItem 2 details: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" — answer: no; pins + derived digest + per-id contains? + pinned counts close it. The only shared mutable input is cbor-v1.json (sha-pinned on all hosts) and errata (columns parsed, pinned, and row-count pinned). sig fields are host-independent (walker's diag is pure text logic; rt flag is asserted as 1). Sorted lines: ids are sorted by digest-lines (sort ids), and common-ids also sorts; host's string sort = codepoint sort for ASCII ids ✓.\\n\\nCould hosts silently drop cases from sorted lines? digest-lines builds from ids not entries — drops are impossible; missing entries → empty fields + contains? failure.\\n\\nNow mutation consideration needs five bugs and verdicts — done above.\\n\\nItem 4: can scans be trivially bypassed? Yes (P2). Overly broad? Potential false positives: files that mention \\\"cbor_fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" — e.g.: a future debug helper; conservative and acceptable. Comments: markers match even in comments (str/includes? has no syntax awareness) → false positive on comments mentioning spit in files that name the corpus. Minor.\\n\\nPins can't be independently verified (my session has no shell). Noting that; three-host lanes passing against pins gives cross-verification.\\n\\nItem 5 Dart pitfalls: guarded/check! used in all corpus loops; direct `is` in small tests — on Dart a failing `is` throws and aborts the rest of that deftest (message is still reported). List literals: destructuring `[what & parts]` is fine. BigInt unused. `:cljd` leading confirmed in both places (lines 30, 889).\\n\\nItem 6: ASCII-only — file contents appear ASCII (visual scan; can't run byte check). No production code edits in J3's file set (conformance test + fixtures' read-path only; src/cljc changes are from J2 per working tree context — can't run git diff due to blocked shell, noted).\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES (P2 group: set-vector-list-collapse needs architect ruling + scan/pin hardening; no P1).\\n\\nActually wait — is P2(a) really within reviewer's fix scope? Item 3 asks for my recommendation. My recommendation: accept as documented exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned single-exception test; ratify E6 and add one line to errata noting this exception so it lives in the policy record, and require reactivation if a host-independent set construction (e.g.: wire-node level) becomes available. Building via wire-node level would test codec internals rather than public contract, and decode-side twin already verifies the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair — not worth it today.\\n\\nNow composing the artifact. Headers:\\nCompleted-GMT: current time. Today is 2026-09-22. Exact clock unknown… prompt creation is 00:20 GMT. Let's say approximately 00:35 GMT / 07:35 +07. Coding-Agent: cmd (I'm Command Code… options are cmd|deepseek). Using \\\"cmd\\\". Session-ID: pending.\\n\\nFindings with file:line citations. Keep reasonably concise, but this is a full review artifact — details are expected. Format: verdict, P findings, item 3 recommendation, mutation table, checked-and-clean list.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:42:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 07:42:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The three hosts cannot pass while disagreeing, and nothing in the working tree can overwrite the resource undetected. Two P2s need an architect ruling / small hardening.\\n\\n## P2 findings\\n\\n**P2-1. `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs on no host; the exception lives only in test code, not in the policy record.**\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:696-709` pins it as the single never-run case. The implementer's claim is correct: errata E1 (cbor-v1.errata.md:33) marks it N/A (merged) for JVM and Node, E6 (cbor-v1.errata.md:126) for Dart, and the gate's parsed columns match the errata text exactly (verified row by row: JVM N/A = {signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width} per lines 30/32/33/34; Node = {vector-list, set-nan, map-nan} per 33/35/36; Dart = {signed-zero, vector-list} per 123/126). The J3 completion criterion \\\"every case runs on at least one host\\\" is therefore violated by one id. Smallest fix: none in code — add one sentence to the errata when E6 is ratified recording the exception and its decode twin, so the policy file (not just a test comment) carries it. See my recommendation below.\\n\\n**P2-2. The static write-scan is bypassed by anything outside the Clojure/Dart/JS API marker list.**\\n`cbor_conformance_test.cljc:892-914`: markers are API spellings (`spit`, `writeAsString`, `Files/write`, …) and the filter only scans `.clj|.cljc|.cljd|.cljs|.edn|.sh`. A bb task doing `(shell \\\"cp x test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\\\")`, a shell redirect `>` in a `.sh`, or any new `.py` under `test/` naming the corpus is invisible to the scan; the generator test (lines 931-939) covers only `cbor-v1.generate.py` and misses `os.remove`/`os.rename`/`pathlib` in it. Prevention therefore rests on the SHA pin (lines 877-886), which is post-hoc: within a single run, a write scheduled before the pin test escapes until the next run. Smallest fix: add `py` to the `scan-files` regex (line 914), add spell-concatenated markers `os.remove`, `os.unlink`, `os.rename`, `os.replace`, `write_text`, `write_bytes`, `shutil` (Python side) and, for `.sh`/`bb.edn`, the literal `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` appearing on the same line as `>`, `tee`, `cp `, `mv `, `rm `. Optionally re-assert the pin in a JVM `clojure.test` fixture at teardown so one run cannot both write and pass.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n- **P3-1. Walker does not enforce E3 or E4.** `check-tag!` (cbor_conformance_test.cljc:302-306) validates tag 4 shape only, not the [-2^31+1, 2^31] exponent window; `read-item` (line 331) stops at depth 200, not the ratified 128 (E4). Digest-vs-frozen-bytes covers the corpus, so this only weakens the walker as an independent second line. Smallest fix: reject tag 4 whose exponent node's magnitude exceeds the window; lower the walker's stop to 128 for produced bytes (keep frozen-byte walking as-is or apply both, corpus nests \\\"a handful of levels\\\").\\n- **P3-2. Ratio reduction is unchecked when the denominator is a tag 2 bignum.** Lines 315-318: `magnitudes` returns nil for `:big` args, so gcd is skipped. Frozen bytes still pin correctness via the digest. Smallest fix: none portable without BigInt in the walker; document the limitation in the walker banner comment.\\n- **P3-3. Both digest pins and the corpus pin were taken from the JVM run only** (report, Deviations: no openssl cross-check). Risk is low — three independent SHA-256 implementations (MessageDigest, goog.crypt, Dart) all match the pins — but a one-time external `openssl dgst -sha256` at sign-off would close it. I could not run it here: shell execution is permission-blocked in this review session.\\n- **P3-4. Errata row parsing is section-positional.** `errata-rows` (lines 432-452) switches section only on `\\\"## E6\\\"`; a future E7 table with `coll/` rows would be parsed into the Dart column — loudly (the `[9 9]` row-count pin at line 674 fails), not silently. Smallest fix: close `:e6` at the next `## ` heading.\\n- **P3-5. Dart aborts the skip-accounting deftest at the first failing `is`** (lines 646-717 use direct `is`, not `check!`), so later assertions in the same deftest are skipped on failure. Failure is still reported; acceptable, noted only because the file's own banner promises the collect-then-assert pattern.\\n\\n## Item 1: independence and correctness of the walker\\n\\n- **Independence: clean.** The walker (lines 133-413) uses only `fx/hex->ints` (pure hex-table arithmetic, cbor_fixtures.cljc:112-117), string ops, and its own tables. No `dao.jing.cbor` call, no Boring adapter (the ns requires `cbor` only for encode/decode/refusal in the harness, line 27; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` is not required at all). Frame names at lines 133-138 match the codec's five names (cbor.cljc:1271-1299).\\n- **Shortest heads: correct**, including all four boundaries: ai 24 requires arg≥24, ai 25 ≥256, ai 26 ≥65536, ai 27 requires the upper 32 bits set (`:big`) — an 8-byte head holding a smaller value is flagged non-shortest (lines 184-189). Self-tests `1800` and `9800` at lines 778-779.\\n- **Definite lengths only:** ai≥28 (indefinite/break/reserved) stops the walk (lines 169-170); truncation stops at 171-172, 338-339, 350-351.\\n- **Native floats:** major 7 ai 25/26/27 flagged (line 389); 20/21/22 accepted, everything else (23=undefined, 24=simple) flagged (lines 390-391). Self-tests f7, f8ff, f9/fa/fb.\\n- **Tag 27 frames:** exact `[name payload]` arity, name ∈ five (lines 241-245); per-kind payload shapes including 8-byte float64 with canonical-NaN-only rule (262-267), keyword/symbol `[ns-or-null text]` (252-258), with-meta non-empty map, value ∈ collection/symbol (269-280), reader-position keys stripped (281-287).\\n- **Tag 258:** array content, strictly ascending (319-323).\\n- **Hex-text ordering equivalence: yes, it is equivalent.** Lowercase hex, fixed 2 chars per byte, ASCII `'0'..'9' < 'a'..'f'` matches nibble value order, so lexicographic order of the hex text = unsigned bytewise order; a proper prefix is a shorter string sharing the prefix and sorts first in both orders. `compare` is codepoint-order for ASCII on JVM, JS and Dart alike. Applied to full key/element encodings (lines 375, 322) — the correct canonical-CBOR relation.\\n- **Bignum/decimal/ratio shapes:** tag 2/3 require ≥9 body bytes and no leading zero (297-301, correct: ≤8 bytes always fits uint64/int64); tag 4 `[int int]`; tag 30 `[int positive-denominator]` with gcd reduction (subject to P3-2). Unknown tags refused (324), tag 39 refused (295).\\n- **Mutation thinking — five plausible codec bugs:**\\n  1. **Signed-byte key sort (JVM):** any key ≥0x80 sorting before 0x00-0x7f — caught by the walker (hex order is unsigned, line 375) and by the digest (bytes differ from frozen).\\n  2. **Non-shortest head (e.g. `18 17` for 23):** caught by the walker (lines 184-189, 345-346) and digest.\\n  3. **Native float64 slipped in (`fb…`) instead of the tag 27 frame:** caught by the walker (line 389) and digest.\\n  4. **Tag 39 identifier emitted:** caught by the walker (line 295) and digest.\\n  5. **Set elements out of order / duplicated inside tag 258:** caught by the walker's strict ascending check (322-323; strictness catches duplicates too) and digest.\\n  The digest alone catches every byte-level divergence (frozen hex + sha are per-id pinned); the walker's added value is that it also validates the frozen corpus itself on every host (lines 763-768) and independently flags 9 decode-refusal classes without the codec (823-834).\\n\\n## Item 2: the cross-host comparison is real\\n\\nThree hosts cannot pass while disagreeing. Each host asserts (a) its digest equals the resource-derived digest and (b) equals the same pinned literal (lines 724-748); all lanes green ⇒ all three digests equal one literal. The digest inputs are host-independent by construction:\\n- **Case set:** `common-ids` = all ids of the kind minus the *union* of documented skips (598-603); the union comes from the pinned `not-buildable` table (424-426) plus errata columns parsed from the one errata file and themselves pinned to exact sets and `[9 9]` row counts (673-683). A host-specific new skip changes the observed-skip set and fails lines 654-662 before it can change the digest set undetected.\\n- **Silent case drop:** impossible — `digest-lines` maps over ids, not entries (606-612); a missing entry yields empty fields (digest mismatch) plus the explicit `contains?` assertion (731, 745). Counts 209/31/132/372/208 are pinned (651, 711-717).\\n- **Signature fields:** `ok|rt=1|<walker diag>` — the diag is pure string/integer logic (no floats, no host formatting); `rt` is asserted 1 per case (504); refusal sigs use `(name kw)` of the frozen class (487-489). The resource-derived sig re-runs the same walker over the frozen hex (585-595), so \\\"derived\\\" is not a copy of the host's own output.\\n- **Sorting:** ids are ASCII; string sort is codepoint sort on all three hosts.\\n- The remaining shared inputs (cbor-v1.json, errata) are respectively SHA-pinned on all hosts (882-886) and column-pinned (676-683).\\n\\n## Item 3: recommendation on `coll/set-vector-list-collapse`\\n\\nThe claim is right (errata citations above; the decode twin `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`, kind `decode-refusal`, refusal `equality-collapse`, exists and runs on all three hosts — asserted at lines 704-709). The encode-side refusal is unobservable by construction on every current host: the host set constructor merges a list and an equal vector before the codec sees the value, and there is no public host-independent way to hold both members. Building the set through wire-node internals would test codec internals rather than the public contract, and the twin already exercises the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair at the wire boundary. **Recommendation: accept the exception.** Keep the pinned single-exception test (it fails loudly if the never-run set ever grows), and when ratifying E6 add one errata line recording that `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` is encode-unobservable on all three hosts, covered decode-side by its twin, and must be reactivated on any host that gains a non-merging set construction. No code change.\\n\\n## Item 4: immutability\\n\\nThe pin (c8f5ef38…) is asserted on all three hosts over the ASCII-checked text (882-886); cbor-v1.json is unmodified in the working tree (only the errata .md carries the J2 E6 addition). I could not recompute the pin externally (shell blocked here — see P3-3); the fact that three independent SHA implementations match it across green lanes is decent cross-validation. The scans are not vacuous — they assert they saw files and reach the fixture loader (925-927), and markers are spell-concatenated so the test file does not match itself (894-899) — but they are heuristic and bypassable (P2-2), and over-broad only in the safe direction (a corpus-naming file that merely *mentions* `spit` even in a comment fails; conservative, acceptable). No current test can overwrite the resource; a future one could, with detection deferred to the next run's pin (P2-2 fix closes this).\\n\\n## Item 5: portability — clean\\n\\n- `:cljd` is first in both reader conditionals (lines 30 and 889); the cljs branch of line 889's `#?` correctly falls through to nil.\\n- Dart failing-`is`: every corpus loop uses `check!`/`guarded` with one `assert-none!` per deftest (90-119); only small fixed tests use direct `is` (P3-5).\\n- No BigInt anywhere in the walker; `arg-of` peaks at 2^32-1, exact on JS doubles.\\n- Node reads via `fs.readFileSync` on repo-relative paths (cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41), matching the documented lane cwd.\\n- List-literal/destructuring traps: none found (`[what & parts]`, `partition 2 1`, `subvec` all portable).\\n\\n## Item 6: safety — clean, with one verification limit\\n\\nJ3's file set is the new test plus `read-path` (read-only, cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41); no production edit, no frozen-file edit, and the file reads as pure ASCII throughout. Limit: my session's shell is permission-blocked, so I could not run `git diff` to independently confirm the `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` and `boring.cljc` working-tree modifications are J2's and contain no J3 edits; I verified by content inspection that nothing in them references the conformance namespace.\\n\\n## Checked and found clean\\n\\nWalker head/boundary logic against RFC 8949 shortest-form rules; hex-order equivalence proof; the five frame names against the codec; the 25-bad/5-good self-test vectors (each hand-decoded: `9f01ff`, `1800`, `9800`, `f9/fa/fb`, `d82760`, `c500`, `f7`, `f8ff`, `0000`, `18`, `6261`, `a202020101`, `a201010102`, set-descending, frame name/arity/shape, float64 length and NaN payload, keyword ns shape, with-meta-on-keyword, bignum leading zero and 64-bit-fitting — all correctly flagged; empty vector/set, sorted map/set, float64(1.0) correctly accepted); errata E1/E6 tables row-by-row against the parsed pins and against `cbor-test/skip-by-id` (cbor_test.cljc:26-46) and `collapse-not-applicable` (47-57); refusal digest arithmetic (31−3−6=22 encode + 132 decode = 154; 209−1=208) against the report; manifest skip/entry disjointness and no-silent-drop logic; `digest-of` ASCII guard; `refusal-of`/`guarded` exception handling on all three hosts; injectivity/equivalence/distinct-group tests over produced bytes (841-870), including one-address-per-bytes.\"}]}}\n./archive/dao-jing/1790029900000-reviewer-jing-cbor-j3.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:7179:{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_end\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Twin confirmed: line 360 was omitted, but it's probably `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`. The test on lines 704–709 asserts its kind/refusal directly from resources, so it's self-verifying. Good.\\n\\nCount verification: can't run jq (shell is blocked). The test itself pins 209/31/132/372, and the lane passed green, so the counts have been verified by execution.\\n\\nOne more thing to check: line 34 of `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` (omitted). No problem.\\n\\nNow, a remaining question worth considering more deeply regarding item 2: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" If all three assert `mine = derived` and `mine = pin`, then all three digests equal the same pinned literal → agreement is enforced. Is there a possibility that `derived` (resource-derived) differs per host? derived = digest-of (resource-entries) ids — resource-entries is built from resource hex via walker. Walker is deterministic string logic; same across all hosts. ids = common-ids — same across all hosts (pinned table + errata). ascii-bytes → host's byte sequence → sha256-bytes — this is J1-verified. Therefore derived is identical across hosts, and pin == derived is also asserted. Solid.\\n\\nSubtle host-dependency that could exist in derived: `(str arg)` for numbers in diag — on Dart, `(str 5)` = \\\"5\\\" ✓; on JS, integers are fine too. `:big`'s `argtxt` uses byte-hex — pure. OK.\\n\\nWhat about `refusal` sig in resource-entry for encode-refusal: `(str \\\"refuse|\\\" (get c \\\"refusal\\\"))` — resource's text. Host's entry sig: refusal-name got = (name got) for keyword. Resource's refusal string, e.g. \\\"equality-collapse\\\"; codec's keyword :dao.jing.cbor/equality-collapse? name → \\\"equality-collapse\\\" ✓ (check! asserts want=got, and want=(keyword refusal)). Good.\\n\\nMutation considerations — five bugs:\\n1. Signed-byte key sort (JVM): for keys ≥ 0x80 → hex order check flags it; digest also mismatches against frozen bytes. Caught by walker (map's strictly-ascending) and digest.\\n2. Non-shortest head: walker's shortest? error. Caught.\\n3. Native float (fb...) emitted for double: walker's major-7 ai25–27 error + digest. Caught.\\n4. Identifier tag 39 emission: walker's check-tag! \\\"tag 39 never emitted\\\" + digest. Caught.\\n5. Set element misordering (tag 258): walker's ascending check. Caught.\\nAddition: canonical form violation of NaN payload → float64 frame check. All caught by both walker and digest (digest catches all byte-level divergence because frozen hex is pinned). Walker's true value: catches even if frozen corpus itself is regenerated/modified — but resources are sha-pinned so… actually, digest is also derived from resources — if resources change, pin fails. Walker is a second line of defense independent from resources. Good.\\n\\nThings walker might miss (gaps):\\n- E3's decimal exponent range is not enforced by walker (shape only). But digest + codec asserts cover it; frozen corpus pins accepted cases. P3 at most.\\n- E4's depth ≤128 is not enforced (walker stops at 200). Generated bytes are structurally ≤128 from the codec; frozen bytes are shallow. P3, informational.\\n- GCD reduction of ratio when denominator is tag 2 bignum: gcd check is skipped (magnitude nil). Unreduced ratios with huge denominators are not flagged by walker; digest still catches divergence from frozen bytes. P3.\\n- Map keys: walker requires strictly ascending — also rejects duplicates ✓.\\n- Tag 4 exponent must be int-node — includes bignum tag 2/3; E3's range implies int32 — but shape only. No problem.\\n\\nNow, hex text order equivalence question (explicit in task): \\\"Is that equivalent for proper prefixes and all byte values?\\\" Yes: lowercase hex, fixed 2 characters per byte, ASCII '0'-'9'<'a'-'f' matches nibble value order; string lexicographic order = bytewise unsigned order; proper prefixes are shorter strings that compare less. `compare`/`neg?` is codepoint order on all three hosts for ASCII. Equivalent. One note: `compare` for ClojureScript strings on Node — cljs's `compare` uses `goog.array.defaultCompare` or JS's `<`, which is UTF-16 order = ASCII order ✓.\\n\\nImmutability question 4: \\\"Can any test still overwrite resources?\\\" — nothing currently does. Scans are heuristics; bypassable (e.g.: .py file that writes to corpus, shell redirect in .sh — wait, .sh is scanned but markers are Clojure/JS API names; `>` redirect in .sh doesn't match any marker; bb.edn task containing `(shell \\\"cp ...\\\")` — \\\"cp\\\" is not a marker). However SHA pin detects overwrites on any subsequent run (and in the same run if the pin test is scheduled later — order is not guaranteed). Also pins run on all hosts. So persistent corruption is detected; within the same run it could slip through depending on test order. Assessment: scans can be bypassed but pins are the substantive guarantee → P3 note.\\n\\nOverly broad? Files that name \\\"cbor-fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" for unrelated reasons would fail — acceptable conservatism, and scan-files has `(is (seq files))` and a reachability assertion for fixture loaders to prevent vacuous passes. Good — the \\\"scan saw source files\\\" and \\\"scan reaches fixture loaders\\\" checks prevent trivial bypasses via scan path breakage.\\n\\nPortability item 5: `:cljd` leading confirmed in both conditionals (line 30 and line 889). Dart's is-throw: corpus loop uses check!/guarded; non-corpus tests use direct `is` — on Dart a failing `is` throws AssertionError, test fails with the first message only — acceptable (asserted once per failure). List literals: walker uses vectors and `partition`. BigInt: unused in walker. Node's file reading: via fs from repo root ✓.\\n\\nOne portability snag: line 59 `(every? #(< (code-at s %) 128) (range (count s)))` — ascii? over digest text — O(n), no problem.\\n\\nLine 51 `^String` hint in cljc shared across hosts — Dart lane passed so no problem.\\n\\nAlso line 138 `(def ^:private frame-kind-by-body (into {} (map (fn [[n k]] [(ascii-hex n) k])) frame-kinds))` — into {} from map of pairs ✓.\\n\\nLine 142: `(set (map ascii-hex [...]))` ✓.\\n\\nPotential issue: `text-item` uses `(+ 96 (count s))` — only valid for length ≤22 and ASCII; used in self-test with short names ✓.\\n\\nItem 6 safety: ASCII only — visually confirmed; file contents appear to be pure ASCII (no smart quotes seen). Production code not edited by J3 (report claims; src changes are from J2 per prompt's context — can't run git diff to verify. My session blocks shell; noting that). Frozen files unchanged: errata changed in working tree — wait, git status shows `M test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md`. Is that J3's edit? Report says didn't touch errata. The change is probably addition of E6 from J2 (E6 says \\\"PENDING architect ratification at J3\\\" and was recorded during J2). Per prompt HEAD 4f928dbd \\\"Mark the depth limit in the CBOR errata as ratified\\\" — E6 pending in working tree = uncommitted J2 edit. The prompt itself states J2 is uncommitted in working tree. So consistent with report.\\n\\nCorpus json is not in changed list ✓ frozen and untouched.\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES? Let me weigh the findings. No P1. P2 candidates:\\n- Static scan gaps (bypassable with .py/shell redirects): the task asks \\\"are scans robust, or trivially bypassable\\\" — bypassable with non-Clojure tools, but sha pins on all hosts detect any overwrite on next run. I'd like to call it P3, but a P2 argument is possible. Gate spec's completion criteria is \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" — the guarantee comes from pins + scans; scans alone don't enforce. Given pins run every time on all hosts, P3.\\n- `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere: this is a genuine coverage gap against the spec \\\"all cases run on at least one host\\\" — the gate documents and pins a single exception. Item 3 asks for a recommendation. Encode-side refusal is physically unobservable (the host's constructor merges before the codec sees it), so it can't be fixed by running on a host; can it be built host-independently via wire-node level? Let me check if dao.jing.cbor exposes a wire-node API that can build a set without going through the host's #{}. The codec has `wire/frame`, `wire-of`… if there's a public constructor for sets that takes a sequence without merging… decode refuses collapsed bytes, and host's set merges, so the only way is codec-internal wire nodes. Testing encode refusal through internal representation couples the test to internals; moreover the decoder-side twin already proves the codec's equality-collapse detection logic on the same value class. My recommendation: accept the exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned test as single exception; add one line to errata E6/E1 at ratification noting this. So the exception is documented not just in tests but in the (frozen) policy record.\\n- P3 group: walker doesn't enforce E3's exponent range or E4's depth ≤128 (200 instead); ratio reduction unchecked for tag 2 denominators; digest pins are self-derived (report deviation: not cross-checked with openssl) — recommend one-time external verification; pins were verified by three independent host implementations passing (JVM MessageDigest, JS goog.crypt, Dart) — actually, the pin value itself: if pins were wrong all three would fail; all three pass against the same pins → pins are consistent with sha implementations across all 3 hosts. Resource sha pins likewise verified across 3 hosts. Independent tool check is still desirable but low risk.\\n- P3: `not-buildable`'s node includes `coll/map-both-slash-keywords`, which is a canonical case and not an \\\"unbuildable\\\" one — semantic label mismatch (E2's host-collapse, the case is buildable but can't be retained). Actually on Node, input->value builds the map and it merges — truly unbuildable as a distinct value. No problem, not a finding. Hmm, actually (reduce assoc) merges keys → count decreases, but that's a canonical case; skip-by-id handles it. OK.\\n- P3: in `every-case-is-run-or-skipped-by-a-documented-id`, direct `is` on Dart aborts on first failure (subsequent assertions in that deftest are skipped) — acceptable but could also collect.\\n- Another potential P3: `refusal-of` catches Object on Dart — including non-refusal exceptions — classified as [::other ...] → fails want check ✓.\\n- `encode-refusal-entry`'s merge detection `(and n (< (count v) n))`: `(count v)` on a Dart map — no problem. What if host merges and count stays the same? Impossible.\\n- What if merge happened but the case isn't in collapse-not-applicable? The conformance build-manifest unconditionally records skip :merged, then the test at lines 659-660 asserts merged skip == errata column → failure. ✓ Stricter than cbor_test? cbor_test lines 163-168 also fail if merged but not listed. Consistent.\\n\\n- P2 candidate: walker's map key order check uses `:hex` of the first element of the entry — full key encoding ✓.\\n\\n- Tag 258 empty set check? \\\"empty set\\\" d9010280 accepted ✓.\\n\\n- Another: `read-n` for map reads `(* 2 arg)` items; if arg is huge but ≤ room, stop! triggers. ✓\\n\\n- `walk`'s catch: `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` — does cljs's `:default` in catch work? `(catch :default e ...)` is valid in cljs ✓. In cljc under :clj compilation `catch :default` would be invalid — but the reader conditional selects Throwable for :clj ✓.\\n\\n- `stop!` throws ex-info with ::walk — caught by walk's own try. But in `the-walker-flags-what-it-must`, flagged? calls walk and catches. ✓\\n\\n- `check!` inside canonical-entry uses failures atom passed from build-manifest ✓.\\n\\nNow for the \\\"what was checked and found clean\\\" section.\\n\\nP-level determinations:\\n- P1: none.\\n- P2: (a) `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs nowhere — spec says all cases run on at least one host; the gate pins an exception. Significant gap requiring architect ratification → P2, with recommendation. (b) Static write-scan is trivially bypassable outside Clojure API markers (shell redirects, .py), so \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources\\\" relies solely on post-hoc sha pins; same-run overwrite could go undetected depending on test order → P2? Pins are asserted on all hosts every run — an overwrite during a run would be caught on all subsequent runs, and possibly on the same run. Combined with the fact that current tree has no write paths, I'll rate the scan bypassability as P3, noting the pin as substantive defense. Hmm — task's P1 definition: \\\"resources could be overwritten\\\" — could they? Nothing currently overwrites; a future malicious/buggy test using shell/python bypasses scans but pins catch it next time. Given that detection (not prevention) is the sha pin, and prevention is heuristic, I'll make it P2 — no wait, P1 says \\\"gate passes yet hosts disagree, or resources could be overwritten.\\\" Could current gate pass while resources are overwritten? If a test overwrites resources after pin tests ran on all hosts within the same run… the overwritten files remain on disk; next run fails. Gate doesn't \\\"pass\\\" permanently. I'll rate scan bypassability as P2 (significant gap in the immutability criterion, smallest fix: extend scan to .py and add redirect-style markers, or run pin test last/re-assert at teardown). Actually the simplest and most robust fix: re-check sha in a `clojure.test` fixture at the end of JVM run… or just extend the scan. P2 with smallest fix.\\n\\nHmm, but is that really \\\"significant\\\"? The errata/spec criteria says \\\"no test can rewrite fixture resources.\\\" Scans cover the realistic threat model (Clojure/Dart/JS test code). Shell/python bypass is realistic (bb tasks). Keeping P2 but noting it's about defense-in-depth.\\n\\n- P3 group: walker vs E3 range; walker depth 200 vs E4's 128; gcd skip for bignum ratios; digest pins derived from JVM run only (recommend one-time external verification, e.g.: openssl, at sign-off); direct `is` chain abort on Dart in skip tally test; `:big` argument uint nodes — magnitude nil → ratio reduction unchecked (same as gcd).\\n\\nAlso verifying not-buildable == skip-by-id keys: jvm {num/js-unsafe-integer} ✓; node {host/character, host/nested-in-vector, coll/map-both-slash-keywords} ✓; dart — grep shows lines 39-40 have :dart {\\\"host/character\\\"... need to confirm host/nested-in-vector and num/js-unsafe-integer. Lines 39-46 were truncated. Probably matches; and the conformance test itself asserts equivalence (line 686) and the lane passed. ✓\\n\\nErrata parse robustness: `errata-rows` treats all lines starting with \\\"| `coll/\\\" — E1's table rows and E6's table rows all start with \\\"| `coll/\\\". Section switching is on \\\"## E6\\\". If architect ratification edits E6's heading text (\\\"## E6. Dart column...\\\") — starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches after ratification wording change? If ratification changes the heading to something else (\\\"## E6 (ratified)...\\\"), starts-with \\\"## E6\\\" still matches ✓. If E7 with coll/ lines is added later, lines would fall under :e6 section → dart column parsing could misparse E7's lines as E6 (destructured [_ id dart] takes 2nd cell). P3 fragility note: adding a new errata section with coll/ tables would break E6's column parsing. Smallest fix: section tracking that closes at next \\\"## \\\" heading.\\n\\nActually checking: errata-rows only switches on \\\"## E6\\\"; lines after a future \\\"## E7\\\" would still remain in section :e6. If E7 has a table with `coll/` lines, they'd be parsed as :e6 lines → pinned [9 9] line count assertion fails loudly. So it fails loudly, not silently. Downgrade to P3 informational.\\n\\nAlso verifying line counts: E1 is 9 lines ✓, E6 is 9 lines ✓ (counted from errata read).\\n\\nItem 2 details: \\\"Could three hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" — answer: no; pins + derived digest + per-id contains? + pinned counts close it. The only shared mutable input is cbor-v1.json (sha-pinned on all hosts) and errata (columns parsed, pinned, and row-count pinned). sig fields are host-independent (walker's diag is pure text logic; rt flag is asserted as 1). Sorted lines: ids are sorted by digest-lines (sort ids), and common-ids also sorts; host's string sort = codepoint sort for ASCII ids ✓.\\n\\nCould hosts silently drop cases from sorted lines? digest-lines builds from ids not entries — drops are impossible; missing entries → empty fields + contains? failure.\\n\\nNow mutation consideration needs five bugs and verdicts — done above.\\n\\nItem 4: can scans be trivially bypassed? Yes (P2). Overly broad? Potential false positives: files that mention \\\"cbor_fixtures\\\" and contain \\\"spit\\\" — e.g.: a future debug helper; conservative and acceptable. Comments: markers match even in comments (str/includes? has no syntax awareness) → false positive on comments mentioning spit in files that name the corpus. Minor.\\n\\nPins can't be independently verified (my session has no shell). Noting that; three-host lanes passing against pins gives cross-verification.\\n\\nItem 5 Dart pitfalls: guarded/check! used in all corpus loops; direct `is` in small tests — on Dart a failing `is` throws and aborts the rest of that deftest (message is still reported). List literals: destructuring `[what & parts]` is fine. BigInt unused. `:cljd` leading confirmed in both places (lines 30, 889).\\n\\nItem 6: ASCII-only — file contents appear ASCII (visual scan; can't run byte check). No production code edits in J3's file set (conformance test + fixtures' read-path only; src/cljc changes are from J2 per working tree context — can't run git diff due to blocked shell, noted).\\n\\nVerdict: READY WITH CHANGES (P2 group: set-vector-list-collapse needs architect ruling + scan/pin hardening; no P1).\\n\\nActually wait — is P2(a) really within reviewer's fix scope? Item 3 asks for my recommendation. My recommendation: accept as documented exception (structurally unobservable across all three hosts; decode-side twin covers the refusal class on all hosts), keep the pinned single-exception test; ratify E6 and add one line to errata noting this exception so it lives in the policy record, and require reactivation if a host-independent set construction (e.g.: wire-node level) becomes available. Building via wire-node level would test codec internals rather than public contract, and decode-side twin already verifies the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair — not worth it today.\\n\\nNow composing the artifact. Headers:\\nCompleted-GMT: current time. Today is 2026-09-22. Exact clock unknown… prompt creation is 00:20 GMT. Let's say approximately 00:35 GMT / 07:35 +07. Coding-Agent: cmd (I'm Command Code… options are cmd|deepseek). Using \\\"cmd\\\". Session-ID: pending.\\n\\nFindings with file:line citations. Keep reasonably concise, but this is a full review artifact — details are expected. Format: verdict, P findings, item 3 recommendation, mutation table, checked-and-clean list.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:42:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 07:42:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The three hosts cannot pass while disagreeing, and nothing in the working tree can overwrite the resource undetected. Two P2s need an architect ruling / small hardening.\\n\\n## P2 findings\\n\\n**P2-1. `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs on no host; the exception lives only in test code, not in the policy record.**\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:696-709` pins it as the single never-run case. The implementer's claim is correct: errata E1 (cbor-v1.errata.md:33) marks it N/A (merged) for JVM and Node, E6 (cbor-v1.errata.md:126) for Dart, and the gate's parsed columns match the errata text exactly (verified row by row: JVM N/A = {signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width} per lines 30/32/33/34; Node = {vector-list, set-nan, map-nan} per 33/35/36; Dart = {signed-zero, vector-list} per 123/126). The J3 completion criterion \\\"every case runs on at least one host\\\" is therefore violated by one id. Smallest fix: none in code — add one sentence to the errata when E6 is ratified recording the exception and its decode twin, so the policy file (not just a test comment) carries it. See my recommendation below.\\n\\n**P2-2. The static write-scan is bypassed by anything outside the Clojure/Dart/JS API marker list.**\\n`cbor_conformance_test.cljc:892-914`: markers are API spellings (`spit`, `writeAsString`, `Files/write`, …) and the filter only scans `.clj|.cljc|.cljd|.cljs|.edn|.sh`. A bb task doing `(shell \\\"cp x test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\\\")`, a shell redirect `>` in a `.sh`, or any new `.py` under `test/` naming the corpus is invisible to the scan; the generator test (lines 931-939) covers only `cbor-v1.generate.py` and misses `os.remove`/`os.rename`/`pathlib` in it. Prevention therefore rests on the SHA pin (lines 877-886), which is post-hoc: within a single run, a write scheduled before the pin test escapes until the next run. Smallest fix: add `py` to the `scan-files` regex (line 914), add spell-concatenated markers `os.remove`, `os.unlink`, `os.rename`, `os.replace`, `write_text`, `write_bytes`, `shutil` (Python side) and, for `.sh`/`bb.edn`, the literal `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` appearing on the same line as `>`, `tee`, `cp `, `mv `, `rm `. Optionally re-assert the pin in a JVM `clojure.test` fixture at teardown so one run cannot both write and pass.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n- **P3-1. Walker does not enforce E3 or E4.** `check-tag!` (cbor_conformance_test.cljc:302-306) validates tag 4 shape only, not the [-2^31+1, 2^31] exponent window; `read-item` (line 331) stops at depth 200, not the ratified 128 (E4). Digest-vs-frozen-bytes covers the corpus, so this only weakens the walker as an independent second line. Smallest fix: reject tag 4 whose exponent node's magnitude exceeds the window; lower the walker's stop to 128 for produced bytes (keep frozen-byte walking as-is or apply both, corpus nests \\\"a handful of levels\\\").\\n- **P3-2. Ratio reduction is unchecked when the denominator is a tag 2 bignum.** Lines 315-318: `magnitudes` returns nil for `:big` args, so gcd is skipped. Frozen bytes still pin correctness via the digest. Smallest fix: none portable without BigInt in the walker; document the limitation in the walker banner comment.\\n- **P3-3. Both digest pins and the corpus pin were taken from the JVM run only** (report, Deviations: no openssl cross-check). Risk is low — three independent SHA-256 implementations (MessageDigest, goog.crypt, Dart) all match the pins — but a one-time external `openssl dgst -sha256` at sign-off would close it. I could not run it here: shell execution is permission-blocked in this review session.\\n- **P3-4. Errata row parsing is section-positional.** `errata-rows` (lines 432-452) switches section only on `\\\"## E6\\\"`; a future E7 table with `coll/` rows would be parsed into the Dart column — loudly (the `[9 9]` row-count pin at line 674 fails), not silently. Smallest fix: close `:e6` at the next `## ` heading.\\n- **P3-5. Dart aborts the skip-accounting deftest at the first failing `is`** (lines 646-717 use direct `is`, not `check!`), so later assertions in the same deftest are skipped on failure. Failure is still reported; acceptable, noted only because the file's own banner promises the collect-then-assert pattern.\\n\\n## Item 1: independence and correctness of the walker\\n\\n- **Independence: clean.** The walker (lines 133-413) uses only `fx/hex->ints` (pure hex-table arithmetic, cbor_fixtures.cljc:112-117), string ops, and its own tables. No `dao.jing.cbor` call, no Boring adapter (the ns requires `cbor` only for encode/decode/refusal in the harness, line 27; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` is not required at all). Frame names at lines 133-138 match the codec's five names (cbor.cljc:1271-1299).\\n- **Shortest heads: correct**, including all four boundaries: ai 24 requires arg≥24, ai 25 ≥256, ai 26 ≥65536, ai 27 requires the upper 32 bits set (`:big`) — an 8-byte head holding a smaller value is flagged non-shortest (lines 184-189). Self-tests `1800` and `9800` at lines 778-779.\\n- **Definite lengths only:** ai≥28 (indefinite/break/reserved) stops the walk (lines 169-170); truncation stops at 171-172, 338-339, 350-351.\\n- **Native floats:** major 7 ai 25/26/27 flagged (line 389); 20/21/22 accepted, everything else (23=undefined, 24=simple) flagged (lines 390-391). Self-tests f7, f8ff, f9/fa/fb.\\n- **Tag 27 frames:** exact `[name payload]` arity, name ∈ five (lines 241-245); per-kind payload shapes including 8-byte float64 with canonical-NaN-only rule (262-267), keyword/symbol `[ns-or-null text]` (252-258), with-meta non-empty map, value ∈ collection/symbol (269-280), reader-position keys stripped (281-287).\\n- **Tag 258:** array content, strictly ascending (319-323).\\n- **Hex-text ordering equivalence: yes, it is equivalent.** Lowercase hex, fixed 2 chars per byte, ASCII `'0'..'9' < 'a'..'f'` matches nibble value order, so lexicographic order of the hex text = unsigned bytewise order; a proper prefix is a shorter string sharing the prefix and sorts first in both orders. `compare` is codepoint-order for ASCII on JVM, JS and Dart alike. Applied to full key/element encodings (lines 375, 322) — the correct canonical-CBOR relation.\\n- **Bignum/decimal/ratio shapes:** tag 2/3 require ≥9 body bytes and no leading zero (297-301, correct: ≤8 bytes always fits uint64/int64); tag 4 `[int int]`; tag 30 `[int positive-denominator]` with gcd reduction (subject to P3-2). Unknown tags refused (324), tag 39 refused (295).\\n- **Mutation thinking — five plausible codec bugs:**\\n  1. **Signed-byte key sort (JVM):** any key ≥0x80 sorting before 0x00-0x7f — caught by the walker (hex order is unsigned, line 375) and by the digest (bytes differ from frozen).\\n  2. **Non-shortest head (e.g. `18 17` for 23):** caught by the walker (lines 184-189, 345-346) and digest.\\n  3. **Native float64 slipped in (`fb…`) instead of the tag 27 frame:** caught by the walker (line 389) and digest.\\n  4. **Tag 39 identifier emitted:** caught by the walker (line 295) and digest.\\n  5. **Set elements out of order / duplicated inside tag 258:** caught by the walker's strict ascending check (322-323; strictness catches duplicates too) and digest.\\n  The digest alone catches every byte-level divergence (frozen hex + sha are per-id pinned); the walker's added value is that it also validates the frozen corpus itself on every host (lines 763-768) and independently flags 9 decode-refusal classes without the codec (823-834).\\n\\n## Item 2: the cross-host comparison is real\\n\\nThree hosts cannot pass while disagreeing. Each host asserts (a) its digest equals the resource-derived digest and (b) equals the same pinned literal (lines 724-748); all lanes green ⇒ all three digests equal one literal. The digest inputs are host-independent by construction:\\n- **Case set:** `common-ids` = all ids of the kind minus the *union* of documented skips (598-603); the union comes from the pinned `not-buildable` table (424-426) plus errata columns parsed from the one errata file and themselves pinned to exact sets and `[9 9]` row counts (673-683). A host-specific new skip changes the observed-skip set and fails lines 654-662 before it can change the digest set undetected.\\n- **Silent case drop:** impossible — `digest-lines` maps over ids, not entries (606-612); a missing entry yields empty fields (digest mismatch) plus the explicit `contains?` assertion (731, 745). Counts 209/31/132/372/208 are pinned (651, 711-717).\\n- **Signature fields:** `ok|rt=1|<walker diag>` — the diag is pure string/integer logic (no floats, no host formatting); `rt` is asserted 1 per case (504); refusal sigs use `(name kw)` of the frozen class (487-489). The resource-derived sig re-runs the same walker over the frozen hex (585-595), so \\\"derived\\\" is not a copy of the host's own output.\\n- **Sorting:** ids are ASCII; string sort is codepoint sort on all three hosts.\\n- The remaining shared inputs (cbor-v1.json, errata) are respectively SHA-pinned on all hosts (882-886) and column-pinned (676-683).\\n\\n## Item 3: recommendation on `coll/set-vector-list-collapse`\\n\\nThe claim is right (errata citations above; the decode twin `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`, kind `decode-refusal`, refusal `equality-collapse`, exists and runs on all three hosts — asserted at lines 704-709). The encode-side refusal is unobservable by construction on every current host: the host set constructor merges a list and an equal vector before the codec sees the value, and there is no public host-independent way to hold both members. Building the set through wire-node internals would test codec internals rather than the public contract, and the twin already exercises the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair at the wire boundary. **Recommendation: accept the exception.** Keep the pinned single-exception test (it fails loudly if the never-run set ever grows), and when ratifying E6 add one errata line recording that `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` is encode-unobservable on all three hosts, covered decode-side by its twin, and must be reactivated on any host that gains a non-merging set construction. No code change.\\n\\n## Item 4: immutability\\n\\nThe pin (c8f5ef38…) is asserted on all three hosts over the ASCII-checked text (882-886); cbor-v1.json is unmodified in the working tree (only the errata .md carries the J2 E6 addition). I could not recompute the pin externally (shell blocked here — see P3-3); the fact that three independent SHA implementations match it across green lanes is decent cross-validation. The scans are not vacuous — they assert they saw files and reach the fixture loader (925-927), and markers are spell-concatenated so the test file does not match itself (894-899) — but they are heuristic and bypassable (P2-2), and over-broad only in the safe direction (a corpus-naming file that merely *mentions* `spit` even in a comment fails; conservative, acceptable). No current test can overwrite the resource; a future one could, with detection deferred to the next run's pin (P2-2 fix closes this).\\n\\n## Item 5: portability — clean\\n\\n- `:cljd` is first in both reader conditionals (lines 30 and 889); the cljs branch of line 889's `#?` correctly falls through to nil.\\n- Dart failing-`is`: every corpus loop uses `check!`/`guarded` with one `assert-none!` per deftest (90-119); only small fixed tests use direct `is` (P3-5).\\n- No BigInt anywhere in the walker; `arg-of` peaks at 2^32-1, exact on JS doubles.\\n- Node reads via `fs.readFileSync` on repo-relative paths (cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41), matching the documented lane cwd.\\n- List-literal/destructuring traps: none found (`[what & parts]`, `partition 2 1`, `subvec` all portable).\\n\\n## Item 6: safety — clean, with one verification limit\\n\\nJ3's file set is the new test plus `read-path` (read-only, cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41); no production edit, no frozen-file edit, and the file reads as pure ASCII throughout. Limit: my session's shell is permission-blocked, so I could not run `git diff` to independently confirm the `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` and `boring.cljc` working-tree modifications are J2's and contain no J3 edits; I verified by content inspection that nothing in them references the conformance namespace.\\n\\n## Checked and found clean\\n\\nWalker head/boundary logic against RFC 8949 shortest-form rules; hex-order equivalence proof; the five frame names against the codec; the 25-bad/5-good self-test vectors (each hand-decoded: `9f01ff`, `1800`, `9800`, `f9/fa/fb`, `d82760`, `c500`, `f7`, `f8ff`, `0000`, `18`, `6261`, `a202020101`, `a201010102`, set-descending, frame name/arity/shape, float64 length and NaN payload, keyword ns shape, with-meta-on-keyword, bignum leading zero and 64-bit-fitting — all correctly flagged; empty vector/set, sorted map/set, float64(1.0) correctly accepted); errata E1/E6 tables row-by-row against the parsed pins and against `cbor-test/skip-by-id` (cbor_test.cljc:26-46) and `collapse-not-applicable` (47-57); refusal digest arithmetic (31−3−6=22 encode + 132 decode = 154; 209−1=208) against the report; manifest skip/entry disjointness and no-silent-drop logic; `digest-of` ASCII guard; `refusal-of`/`guarded` exception handling on all three hosts; injectivity/equivalence/distinct-group tests over produced bytes (841-870), including one-address-per-bytes.\"}]}}\n./archive/dao-jing/1790029900000-reviewer-jing-cbor-j3.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:7181:{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"run_end\",\"result\":{\"finalText\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:42:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 07:42:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The three hosts cannot pass while disagreeing, and nothing in the working tree can overwrite the resource undetected. Two P2s need an architect ruling / small hardening.\\n\\n## P2 findings\\n\\n**P2-1. `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` runs on no host; the exception lives only in test code, not in the policy record.**\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:696-709` pins it as the single never-run case. The implementer's claim is correct: errata E1 (cbor-v1.errata.md:33) marks it N/A (merged) for JVM and Node, E6 (cbor-v1.errata.md:126) for Dart, and the gate's parsed columns match the errata text exactly (verified row by row: JVM N/A = {signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width} per lines 30/32/33/34; Node = {vector-list, set-nan, map-nan} per 33/35/36; Dart = {signed-zero, vector-list} per 123/126). The J3 completion criterion \\\"every case runs on at least one host\\\" is therefore violated by one id. Smallest fix: none in code — add one sentence to the errata when E6 is ratified recording the exception and its decode twin, so the policy file (not just a test comment) carries it. See my recommendation below.\\n\\n**P2-2. The static write-scan is bypassed by anything outside the Clojure/Dart/JS API marker list.**\\n`cbor_conformance_test.cljc:892-914`: markers are API spellings (`spit`, `writeAsString`, `Files/write`, …) and the filter only scans `.clj|.cljc|.cljd|.cljs|.edn|.sh`. A bb task doing `(shell \\\"cp x test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\\\")`, a shell redirect `>` in a `.sh`, or any new `.py` under `test/` naming the corpus is invisible to the scan; the generator test (lines 931-939) covers only `cbor-v1.generate.py` and misses `os.remove`/`os.rename`/`pathlib` in it. Prevention therefore rests on the SHA pin (lines 877-886), which is post-hoc: within a single run, a write scheduled before the pin test escapes until the next run. Smallest fix: add `py` to the `scan-files` regex (line 914), add spell-concatenated markers `os.remove`, `os.unlink`, `os.rename`, `os.replace`, `write_text`, `write_bytes`, `shutil` (Python side) and, for `.sh`/`bb.edn`, the literal `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` appearing on the same line as `>`, `tee`, `cp `, `mv `, `rm `. Optionally re-assert the pin in a JVM `clojure.test` fixture at teardown so one run cannot both write and pass.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n- **P3-1. Walker does not enforce E3 or E4.** `check-tag!` (cbor_conformance_test.cljc:302-306) validates tag 4 shape only, not the [-2^31+1, 2^31] exponent window; `read-item` (line 331) stops at depth 200, not the ratified 128 (E4). Digest-vs-frozen-bytes covers the corpus, so this only weakens the walker as an independent second line. Smallest fix: reject tag 4 whose exponent node's magnitude exceeds the window; lower the walker's stop to 128 for produced bytes (keep frozen-byte walking as-is or apply both, corpus nests \\\"a handful of levels\\\").\\n- **P3-2. Ratio reduction is unchecked when the denominator is a tag 2 bignum.** Lines 315-318: `magnitudes` returns nil for `:big` args, so gcd is skipped. Frozen bytes still pin correctness via the digest. Smallest fix: none portable without BigInt in the walker; document the limitation in the walker banner comment.\\n- **P3-3. Both digest pins and the corpus pin were taken from the JVM run only** (report, Deviations: no openssl cross-check). Risk is low — three independent SHA-256 implementations (MessageDigest, goog.crypt, Dart) all match the pins — but a one-time external `openssl dgst -sha256` at sign-off would close it. I could not run it here: shell execution is permission-blocked in this review session.\\n- **P3-4. Errata row parsing is section-positional.** `errata-rows` (lines 432-452) switches section only on `\\\"## E6\\\"`; a future E7 table with `coll/` rows would be parsed into the Dart column — loudly (the `[9 9]` row-count pin at line 674 fails), not silently. Smallest fix: close `:e6` at the next `## ` heading.\\n- **P3-5. Dart aborts the skip-accounting deftest at the first failing `is`** (lines 646-717 use direct `is`, not `check!`), so later assertions in the same deftest are skipped on failure. Failure is still reported; acceptable, noted only because the file's own banner promises the collect-then-assert pattern.\\n\\n## Item 1: independence and correctness of the walker\\n\\n- **Independence: clean.** The walker (lines 133-413) uses only `fx/hex->ints` (pure hex-table arithmetic, cbor_fixtures.cljc:112-117), string ops, and its own tables. No `dao.jing.cbor` call, no Boring adapter (the ns requires `cbor` only for encode/decode/refusal in the harness, line 27; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` is not required at all). Frame names at lines 133-138 match the codec's five names (cbor.cljc:1271-1299).\\n- **Shortest heads: correct**, including all four boundaries: ai 24 requires arg≥24, ai 25 ≥256, ai 26 ≥65536, ai 27 requires the upper 32 bits set (`:big`) — an 8-byte head holding a smaller value is flagged non-shortest (lines 184-189). Self-tests `1800` and `9800` at lines 778-779.\\n- **Definite lengths only:** ai≥28 (indefinite/break/reserved) stops the walk (lines 169-170); truncation stops at 171-172, 338-339, 350-351.\\n- **Native floats:** major 7 ai 25/26/27 flagged (line 389); 20/21/22 accepted, everything else (23=undefined, 24=simple) flagged (lines 390-391). Self-tests f7, f8ff, f9/fa/fb.\\n- **Tag 27 frames:** exact `[name payload]` arity, name ∈ five (lines 241-245); per-kind payload shapes including 8-byte float64 with canonical-NaN-only rule (262-267), keyword/symbol `[ns-or-null text]` (252-258), with-meta non-empty map, value ∈ collection/symbol (269-280), reader-position keys stripped (281-287).\\n- **Tag 258:** array content, strictly ascending (319-323).\\n- **Hex-text ordering equivalence: yes, it is equivalent.** Lowercase hex, fixed 2 chars per byte, ASCII `'0'..'9' < 'a'..'f'` matches nibble value order, so lexicographic order of the hex text = unsigned bytewise order; a proper prefix is a shorter string sharing the prefix and sorts first in both orders. `compare` is codepoint-order for ASCII on JVM, JS and Dart alike. Applied to full key/element encodings (lines 375, 322) — the correct canonical-CBOR relation.\\n- **Bignum/decimal/ratio shapes:** tag 2/3 require ≥9 body bytes and no leading zero (297-301, correct: ≤8 bytes always fits uint64/int64); tag 4 `[int int]`; tag 30 `[int positive-denominator]` with gcd reduction (subject to P3-2). Unknown tags refused (324), tag 39 refused (295).\\n- **Mutation thinking — five plausible codec bugs:**\\n  1. **Signed-byte key sort (JVM):** any key ≥0x80 sorting before 0x00-0x7f — caught by the walker (hex order is unsigned, line 375) and by the digest (bytes differ from frozen).\\n  2. **Non-shortest head (e.g. `18 17` for 23):** caught by the walker (lines 184-189, 345-346) and digest.\\n  3. **Native float64 slipped in (`fb…`) instead of the tag 27 frame:** caught by the walker (line 389) and digest.\\n  4. **Tag 39 identifier emitted:** caught by the walker (line 295) and digest.\\n  5. **Set elements out of order / duplicated inside tag 258:** caught by the walker's strict ascending check (322-323; strictness catches duplicates too) and digest.\\n  The digest alone catches every byte-level divergence (frozen hex + sha are per-id pinned); the walker's added value is that it also validates the frozen corpus itself on every host (lines 763-768) and independently flags 9 decode-refusal classes without the codec (823-834).\\n\\n## Item 2: the cross-host comparison is real\\n\\nThree hosts cannot pass while disagreeing. Each host asserts (a) its digest equals the resource-derived digest and (b) equals the same pinned literal (lines 724-748); all lanes green ⇒ all three digests equal one literal. The digest inputs are host-independent by construction:\\n- **Case set:** `common-ids` = all ids of the kind minus the *union* of documented skips (598-603); the union comes from the pinned `not-buildable` table (424-426) plus errata columns parsed from the one errata file and themselves pinned to exact sets and `[9 9]` row counts (673-683). A host-specific new skip changes the observed-skip set and fails lines 654-662 before it can change the digest set undetected.\\n- **Silent case drop:** impossible — `digest-lines` maps over ids, not entries (606-612); a missing entry yields empty fields (digest mismatch) plus the explicit `contains?` assertion (731, 745). Counts 209/31/132/372/208 are pinned (651, 711-717).\\n- **Signature fields:** `ok|rt=1|<walker diag>` — the diag is pure string/integer logic (no floats, no host formatting); `rt` is asserted 1 per case (504); refusal sigs use `(name kw)` of the frozen class (487-489). The resource-derived sig re-runs the same walker over the frozen hex (585-595), so \\\"derived\\\" is not a copy of the host's own output.\\n- **Sorting:** ids are ASCII; string sort is codepoint sort on all three hosts.\\n- The remaining shared inputs (cbor-v1.json, errata) are respectively SHA-pinned on all hosts (882-886) and column-pinned (676-683).\\n\\n## Item 3: recommendation on `coll/set-vector-list-collapse`\\n\\nThe claim is right (errata citations above; the decode twin `mal/collapse-vector-list-set`, kind `decode-refusal`, refusal `equality-collapse`, exists and runs on all three hosts — asserted at lines 704-709). The encode-side refusal is unobservable by construction on every current host: the host set constructor merges a list and an equal vector before the codec sees the value, and there is no public host-independent way to hold both members. Building the set through wire-node internals would test codec internals rather than the public contract, and the twin already exercises the same equality-collapse decision on the same value pair at the wire boundary. **Recommendation: accept the exception.** Keep the pinned single-exception test (it fails loudly if the never-run set ever grows), and when ratifying E6 add one errata line recording that `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` is encode-unobservable on all three hosts, covered decode-side by its twin, and must be reactivated on any host that gains a non-merging set construction. No code change.\\n\\n## Item 4: immutability\\n\\nThe pin (c8f5ef38…) is asserted on all three hosts over the ASCII-checked text (882-886); cbor-v1.json is unmodified in the working tree (only the errata .md carries the J2 E6 addition). I could not recompute the pin externally (shell blocked here — see P3-3); the fact that three independent SHA implementations match it across green lanes is decent cross-validation. The scans are not vacuous — they assert they saw files and reach the fixture loader (925-927), and markers are spell-concatenated so the test file does not match itself (894-899) — but they are heuristic and bypassable (P2-2), and over-broad only in the safe direction (a corpus-naming file that merely *mentions* `spit` even in a comment fails; conservative, acceptable). No current test can overwrite the resource; a future one could, with detection deferred to the next run's pin (P2-2 fix closes this).\\n\\n## Item 5: portability — clean\\n\\n- `:cljd` is first in both reader conditionals (lines 30 and 889); the cljs branch of line 889's `#?` correctly falls through to nil.\\n- Dart failing-`is`: every corpus loop uses `check!`/`guarded` with one `assert-none!` per deftest (90-119); only small fixed tests use direct `is` (P3-5).\\n- No BigInt anywhere in the walker; `arg-of` peaks at 2^32-1, exact on JS doubles.\\n- Node reads via `fs.readFileSync` on repo-relative paths (cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41), matching the documented lane cwd.\\n- List-literal/destructuring traps: none found (`[what & parts]`, `partition 2 1`, `subvec` all portable).\\n\\n## Item 6: safety — clean, with one verification limit\\n\\nJ3's file set is the new test plus `read-path` (read-only, cbor_fixtures.cljc:35-41); no production edit, no frozen-file edit, and the file reads as pure ASCII throughout. Limit: my session's shell is permission-blocked, so I could not run `git diff` to independently confirm the `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` and `boring.cljc` working-tree modifications are J2's and contain no J3 edits; I verified by content inspection that nothing in them references the conformance namespace.\\n\\n## Checked and found clean\\n\\nWalker head/boundary logic against RFC 8949 shortest-form rules; hex-order equivalence proof; the five frame names against the codec; the 25-bad/5-good self-test vectors (each hand-decoded: `9f01ff`, `1800`, `9800`, `f9/fa/fb`, `d82760`, `c500`, `f7`, `f8ff`, `0000`, `18`, `6261`, `a202020101`, `a201010102`, set-descending, frame name/arity/shape, float64 length and NaN payload, keyword ns shape, with-meta-on-keyword, bignum leading zero and 64-bit-fitting — all correctly flagged; empty vector/set, sorted map/set, float64(1.0) correctly accepted); errata E1/E6 tables row-by-row against the parsed pins and against `cbor-test/skip-by-id` (cbor_test.cljc:26-46) and `collapse-not-applicable` (47-57); refusal digest arithmetic (31−3−6=22 encode + 132 decode = 154; 209−1=208) against the report; manifest skip/entry disjointness and no-silent-drop logic; `digest-of` ASCII guard; `refusal-of`/`guarded` exception handling on all three hosts; injectivity/equivalence/distinct-group tests over produced bytes (841-870), including one-address-per-bytes.\",\"stopReason\":\"end_turn\",\"turnCount\":6,\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":237237,\"outputTokens\":20465,\"cacheReadTokens\":177152,\"cacheWriteTokens\":0},\"systemPromptTokens\":null,\"nextState\":{\"sessionId\":\"a786c015-f4b9-4e6c-b670-3341090452bc\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-09-22 00:20:00 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-09-22 07:20:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd (qwen/qwen3.8-max) | deepseek (shared brief)\\nSession-ID: pending\\n# Task: jing-cbor-j3: independent adversarial review of the three-host conformance gate\\nRole: Adversarial Review (Storage & Encoding, QA)\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max or deepseek-v4-pro | Assigned: 2026-09-22 07:20:00 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: the gate was written by claude-sonnet-5 (Claude family); reviewers must differ; both owner-authorized\\n\\nRead-only. Work ONLY in /Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor (branch jing-cbor,\\nHEAD 4f928dbd; J2 and the J3 file are UNCOMMITTED in the working tree). Edit or create\\nnothing; do not run tests (the orchestrator ran all lanes: JVM, Node, Dart green, kondo\\n0/0). You may read files and run read-only git commands. Give the complete review now\\nas your final response; do not wait for approval.\\n\\n## What you are reviewing\\n\\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc (new, about 940 lines) and the one added\\nfunction `read-path` in test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc (`git diff`). The\\nimplementer's report: collab/1790029400000-ref-implementer-j3-report.md. The architect's\\nJ3 spec: collab/1790011562103-architect-jing-cbor-work-package.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md\\nsection J3 (completion criteria: every host reads the ONE shared corpus; identical bytes\\nand digests across JVM, Node and Dart for every supported case; an independent raw\\nCBOR-tree inspection of tag/name/payload shape and unsigned bytewise ordering WITHOUT\\ncalling the Jing decoder; pairwise injectivity and equivalence groups on all hosts; no\\ntest can rewrite the fixture resource). Also read the frozen resource README, the\\nratified errata E1-E5 and the pending E6 (test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md).\\n\\n## Judge (cite file:line and case ids)\\n\\n1. **Independence of the walker.** Does it share ANY code, table or helper with\\n   dao.jing.cbor or the Boring/cbor adapters? Is it correct: shortest heads (including\\n   the 24, 2