path, mode='w')\\\" has 'w' → match. What about `pathlib.Path.write_text`? Checked via \\\"write_text\\\". `os.remove`? Not checked! Generators could delete with os.remove/os.unlink/rename — not covered. But generators are frozen files; the concern is future tests overwriting resources. The scan covers test/, src/dev, bb.edn for clj-family + edn + sh files. Python scripts elsewhere? A new Python script in test/ wouldn't be scanned (.py not in filter). A new .py file that names \\\"cbor-v1\\\" and writes to it would bypass. Also shell writes via `echo >` in bb tasks — .sh and bb.edn are scanned but markers are Clojure/JS/Dart API names, not shell redirections (\\\">\\\", \\\"tee\\\"). A bb.edn task with `(spit ...)` would be caught; shell `cp foo test/resources/...` in a .sh file — \\\"io/copy\\\" marker wouldn't match bare `cp`. So the scan is heuristic, trivially bypassable by shell redirection or non-Clojure tooling. But is that P-level? The main defense is the SHA pin running on all hosts on every test run — overwriting would fail the gate. Static scan is belt-and-suspenders. I'd rate this P3: scan is heuristic (misses shell redirection, .py files, os.remove in generators); the pinned digest is the real defense. Also scan excludes test/resources/ so frozen generator isn't double-scanned, no problem.\\n\\n   Can any test overwrite the resource? If a test writes, the pin fails (on that run, after the write — but the pin test could run before the writing test in the same run, and destruction is detected on next run). Detection is after the fact. Also nothing in the current tree does this. Acceptable; note it.\\n\\n   Overly broad/false positives: files that name \\\"cbor-v1\\\" and legitimately contain \\\"spit\\\" would fail — e.g., a future helper that writes something else. Possible false positive but conservative direction. No problem.\\n\\n9. **Portability**:\\n   - `#?@(:cljd [] :clj [[clojure.java.io :as io]])` — wait, ordering: cljd first, then clj, then implicit default? Line 30: `#?@(:cljd [] :clj [[clojure.java.io :as io]])` — for cljs both are false → nothing. OK.\\n   - JVM-only block at line 889: `#?(:cljd nil :clj (do ...))` — cljd first ✓. But wait — for cljs (:node), the reader conditional has :cljd nil, :clj (do...), and no :cljs branch → cljs gets… `#?` with no matching branch and no :default evaluates to nil. Good.\\n   - Inside JVM-only do: uses `io/file`, `file-seq`, `slurp`. Gated. No problem.\\n   - `code-at` uses `^String` type hint — in cljc, `^String` hint… in cljs the String hint is fine. In cljd (Dart), `^String` — does ClojureDart support type hints? `(int (.charAt s (int i)))` is :clj branch only. The ^String hint is on the parameter in all branches. Does ClojureDart accept ^String hint? It might resolve String as Dart's String type — since the report says Dart lane passed, probably fine.\\n   - `(int i)` on :clj for charAt — no problem.\\n   - Dart: failing `is` throws — handled by check!/failures pattern; assert-none! is called once per deftest. Inside `every-case-is-run-or-skipped-by-a-documented-id`, multiple `is` directly in the let body — on Dart the first failure throws and rest are skipped, but that's still a test failure; acceptable (they wanted collected failures for corpus loops).\\n   - `strictly-ascending?` uses partition 2 1 — lazy, no problem.\\n   - BigInt: walker uses only fixnums; arg values are at most 2^32-1 via arg-of — no problem. On Dart, numbers are int — no problem.\\n   - Node: read-path uses fs.readFileSync with relative path — lane runs from repo root as documented. ✓\\n   - `println` — available on all hosts.\\n   - Line 83: `(catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e ...)` ✓.\\n   - Line 104: `#?(:cljd (str e) :default (ex-message e))` — cljs ex-message OK.\\n   - byte-hex on negatives? Input is always 0-255. ascii-hex is code units <128, guaranteed by caller? ascii-bytes called on digest text after ascii? check… digest-of checks ascii? first; the-resource-bytes test asserts ascii? then computes. text-item is called on ASCII literals. OK.\\n   - `arg-of` with reduce on [] → 0; subvec of ab with width… for ai=27, ab is 8 bytes; `(subvec ab 0 4)` no problem.\\n   - Potential Dart trap: `(case ai 24 1 25 2 26 4 27 8 0)` — case with fallthrough? Clojure's `case` is fine.\\n   - `(when (<= 25 ai 27) ...)` ✓.\\n   - `enc` closure uses i and %; no problem.\\n\\n   One real concern: `read-item`'s recursion `read-n` uses loop/recur with acc — depth is guarded at 200. Frozen corpus depth is limited. No problem.\\n\\n   `walk` is also called on resource's hex in `resource-entry` for the digest derivation — so the \\\"resource-derived\\\" digest also passes through the walker: if walker has a bug producing host-dependent diag… diag is pure string manipulation; deterministic across hosts. Numbers in diag: `(str arg)` of integers — same across hosts. `argtxt` for :big: hex text. No problem.\\n\\n   Hmm wait, one host-dependence risk in digest: sig `ok|rt=1|diag`. rt? is host-computed; if all hosts pass check, all are true. If one host has rt?=false, it records failure and digest differs → caught by pin. ✓\\n\\n   Another: `refusal-name` for keywords — `want` from resource via `(keyword refusal)`. got from cbor/refusal — keyword. (name got) is host-independent. ✓\\n\\n   Digest lines use \\\"\\\\t\\\" and \\\"\\\\n\\\" — ASCII, no problem. `jing/sha256-bytes` — needs to return lowercase hex string on all hosts. This is J1 code, presumably already tested. Let me quickly check.\\n\\n10. **Correctness question**: `common-ids` for refusal: `(into (common-ids \\\"encode-refusal\\\") (common-ids \\\"decode-refusal\\\"))` — then digest-lines sorts ids. Into of two vectors, sorted. ✓ n=154.\\n\\n11. Verify `(is (= 372 (count ids) (count (set ids))))` — 3-arity is: (= 372 count-ids count-set) ✓.\\n\\n12. `every-case-is-run-or-skipped`: `(is (empty? (filter run skipped)))` — filter of a set by a predicate that's also a set → skipped ∩ run.