scii-bytes` uses fx/hex->bytes → returns host byte arrays. sha256-bytes is OK cross-host.\\n\\n5. Subtle point in `canonical-entry`: `sig` uses `(:diag w)` — diag is produced by the walker over the generated bytes. The resource-derived sig uses the walker over the frozen hex. If the generated hex == frozen hex (already asserted), then the diags would be equal anyway. But actually, if they differ, both check! failure and digest mismatch → double coverage. OK.\\n\\n6. Question about walker correctness: the `:big` argument in major type 0/1 (uint/nint with 8-byte header ≥ 2^64): nodes with :arg :big; magnitude returns nil; the :hex comparison for map keys still works. uint-nodes with :big argument — int-node? includes them. No problem. diag's \\\"u0x...\\\" is also fine.\\n\\n7. ai=27 major 0 with upper 32 bits being zero: e.g. `1b0000000064` — arg=100, shortest? = (= :big arg) = false → flagged as non-shortest. Correct.\\n\\nWait, let me check read-head's shortest? case 27: `(= :big arg)`. If arg is numeric (fits in 32 bits) → false → err. If :big → true. Correct.\\n\\n8. Map key ordering check: `(strictly-ascending? (map (comp :hex first) entries))` — :hex of the key node = the full encoding of the key (enc e covers from i to e — wait, the `enc` closure is `#(subs hex (* 2 i) (* 2 %))` and i is the item's starting offset. In read-item for major 5, entries are read via read-n from `end` — each key node's :hex = its own encoding. ✓\\n\\n9. Hex text ordering equivalence issue (from task item 1): comparing lowercase hex text of byte strings — for equal-length encodings, lexicographic order = byte order. Because nibble characters are ordered by value. Proper prefixes sort first in both. Equivalent. One caveat: `compare` on JVM strings is lexicographic by UTF-16 — same for ASCII. ✓\\n\\n10. Tag 258 sets: does the profile require set elements to be sorted too — check ✓. Does it require no duplicates — strict ascending catches duplicates ✓.\\n\\n11. with-meta rules: errata/README — what are the with-meta rules? check-frame! requires non-empty meta, value ∈ {array, map, tag258, list frame, symbol frame}, and removal of reader-position keys. The test expects \\\"with-meta on a keyword\\\" to be flagged. Is this correct per the profile? Need to check the README/codec: does the codec allow with-meta on keywords? Let me grep the codec for with-meta rules to confirm the walker aligns with the profile — risk of the walker diverging from the actual profile (false-positive flag would fail the lane; the lane passed, so the walker is at least consistent with the corpus). But a walker check that's too strict should fail on the frozen corpus — it passed. Too loose is a risk, but the mutation tests show coverage.\\n\\nActually, there's a potential walker gap: the walker doesn't validate with-meta on keyword frames — per the test, with-meta is only allowed on collections and symbols. The lane passed, so no issue.\\n\\n12. `check-frame!` keyword payload: [ns-or-null name-text] — ns can be text or null ✓. But should the ns text be non-empty? Minor.\\n\\n13. E4 max-depth 128: the walker stops at 200 — looser than the codec, but the walker isn't enforcing codec policy, it's checking wire shape. Corpus depth is < 200. No problem (the walker might accept items that the codec would reject at depth 129-200 — but the walker only inspects codec output; codec output never exceeds 128. Not a gap for generated bytes; for flagging frozen decode-refusals, depth cases aren't included in `seen`. OK).\\n\\n14. Item 2 concern — \\\"could 3 hosts each pass yet disagree with each other?\\\" The pinned literals force all hosts to the same digest; the digest lines contain id, hex, sha, sig. If a host computes against a different set — the contains? assertion + pinned count (208/154 via common-ids, and lines 711-717 pin 209/31/132 and 208). common-ids derivation: all IDs of that kind minus union of documented skips — the documented skips are pinned literals + errata file (same file, same digest... wait, is the errata file itself pinned? The corpus json's sha is pinned, but the errata md isn't pinned. If a host reads a different errata file (working directory divergence), the skips could change — but all lanes run from the repo root, same file. However, nothing pins the errata bytes; the pinned N/A set (lines 676-683) is asserted equal to the parsed errata, so a changed errata would fail. ✓ Effectively pinned through the parsed values.\\n\\nCould a host silently drop cases from sorted lines? digest-lines maps over ids (from common-ids) and does (get entries id) — missing entry → nil field → \\\"id\\\\tnil\\\\tnil\\\\tnil\\\"? Actually (:hex nil) → nil, (str nil) → \\\"\\\" — the line would be \\\"id\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\" → digest mismatch. Plus the explicit contains? check. ✓\\n\\nIs the sig field host-dependent? sig = \\\"ok|rt=1|\\\" + walker's diag — the walker is pure string/integer logic, deterministic. One risk: number formatting via `(str arg)` — args up to 2^32-1; same across hosts. On Dart, `(str 100)` = \\\"100\\\". ✓. diag for float64 frames: hex of byte text. ✓. Map diag \\\"{k:v,...}\\\" order = wire order. Deterministic.\\n\\nAnother one: `refusal-name` — keywords are equal. ✓\\n\\n15. Skip tallies vs. errata: verified above. ✓ The `:rows [9 9]` check also guards against table drift.\\n\\n16. Immutability: the pin is asserted across all hosts (lines 882-886). The pin itself is derived from the JVM run (report §Deviations) — I couldn't independently recompute it (shell blocked in my session). The frozen commit f5f71e95 exists; the orchestrator's lane was green. Note: the pin's correctness depends on the file not having been modified between the freeze and J3 — is the errata file modified in the working tree? git status shows `M test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md` (E6 additions) but cbor-v1.json is unmodified. ✓ The json is unchanged from the freeze.\\n\\nStatic scan weakness (P3):\\n- Only scans .clj/.cljc/.cljd/.cljs/