\\n20. Injectivity test on generated bytes: same as J1 test but over the manifest. Note: `hex-of` on cases not in entries → nil grouping... filtered with `contains? entries`. ✓\\n\\n21. `:sig` check for canonical — the resource-derived sig hardcodes rt=1. If on some host round-tripping fails, the entry's sig is rt=0 → digest mismatch + check! failure ✓.\\n\\n22. ASCII check: the task requires ASCII only for test files. Scanned visually; assume yes. The report claims pure ASCII. Can't run a check. Lines look ASCII.\\n\\n23. Safety: no production edits by J3 — can't run git diff (shell blocked). git status shows src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc and boring.cljc are modified — per the prompt those are J2's (uncommitted). The report claims J3 touched only 2 test files. Could partially verify by checking whether cbor.cljc contains anything conformance-related... that's not proof. Note the constraint.\\n\\nHmm, wait — one more thing to double-check: task item 2 asks \\\"is the digest's 'same case set' derived identically on all hosts from documented skips, or could host-specific skips undetectedly change the set?\\\" common-ids removes the union across hosts — host-independent, derived from pinned not-buildable + parsed errata (asserted pinned). If the errata file were tampered with per-host... same repo file. If a host-specific skip changed (e.g., a new merge on Dart), the observed-skip test would fail, and that case would be in entries/skips — skips would differ from documented → failure. But could a host-specific skip change the digest set undetected? The digest set = all IDs minus union of documented skips. A new undocumented skip on any host → documented-skips test fails on that host. So no. ✓\\n\\nAlso the union-of-skips test at lines 710-713: canonical skips everywhere = only map-both-slash-keywords; 208 common canonical ✓.\\n\\n24. Potential P3: `read-head`'s shortest? case 24 `(>= arg 24)` — arg here is the byte's value; correct. But case 25 `(>= arg 256)`: an ai=25 header with arg in [24,255] is shortest (16-bit needed for 24..255? Actually, the shortest form for 24..255 is ai=24 with 1 byte; ai=25 requires arg≥256, and args 24-255 in 16-bit are non-shortest). ✓ Correct.\\n\\n25. Another walker check: does it reject ai=24 with arg<24? Yes, via the shortest check (err, not stop) — non-fatal error, gets flagged ✓.\\n\\n26. Tag 27 frames: the five names match frame-kinds — \\\"dao.jing/list\\\" \\\"dao.jing/keyword\\\" \\\"dao.jing/symbol\\\" \\\"dao.jing/float64\\\" \\\"clojure/with-meta\\\". Should cross-reference with the codec's name table — grep the codec.\\n\\n27. frame-kind-by-body uses ascii-hex of the name — text-item style. In frame-kind, matches the name node's :body against the hex bodies. ✓\\n\\nLet me quickly grep the codec's frame names and the README's Obligations for cross-checking, and verify the twin cases and counts in the corpus json (grep for IDs).\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"A few cross-checks against the codec, README, and the corpus itself.\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_dd5801877d2a483a89d6ac9f\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"dao\\\\.jing/list|dao\\\\.jing/keyword|dao\\\\.jing/symbol|dao\\\\.jing/float64|clojure/with-meta\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\",\"output_mode\":\"content\",\"head_limit\":20}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_b6a6d204db97417cbdad623d\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"id\\\": \\\"(mal/collapse-vector-list-set|coll/set-vector-list-collapse)\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\",\"output_mode\":\"content\",\"-A\":6}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1790036415665,\"messageId\":\"15f36853-78bc-4406-9d01-c5fd6be77495\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_dd5801877d2a483a89d6ac9f\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1